.SS bad\-super\-call / E1003
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBBad first argument %r given to super()\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when another argument than the current class is given as first argument of the super builtin.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Animal:
    pass


class Tree:
    pass


class Cat(Animal):
    def __init__(self):
        super(Tree, self).__init__()  # [bad\-super\-call]
        super(Animal, self).__init__()

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Animal:
    pass


class Tree:
    pass


class Cat(Animal):
    def __init__(self):
        super(Animal, self).__init__()

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
In Python 2.7, \fBsuper()\fP has to be called with its own class and \fBself\fP as arguments (\fBsuper(Cat, self)\fP), which can
lead to a mix up of parent and child class in the code.
.sp
In Python 3 the recommended way is to call \fBsuper()\fP without arguments (see also \fBsuper\-with\-arguments\fP).
.sp
One exception is calling \fBsuper()\fP on a non\-direct parent class. This can be used to get a method other than the default
method returned by the \fBmro()\fP\&.
.sp
\fBRelated links:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Documentation for super() \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:functions\:.html#\:super>
.UNINDENT
.sp
