.SS possibly\-used\-before\-assignment / E0606
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBPossibly using variable %r before assignment\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIEmitted when a local variable is accessed before its assignment took place in both branches of an if/else switch.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def check_lunchbox(items: list[str]):
    if not items:
        empty = True
    print(empty)  # [possibly\-used\-before\-assignment]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def check_lunchbox(items: list[str]):
    empty = False
    if not items:
        empty = True
    print(empty)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
You can use \fBassert_never\fP to mark exhaustive choices:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from typing import assert_never

def handle_date_suffix(suffix):
    if suffix == \(dqd\(dq:
        ...
    elif suffix == \(dqm\(dq:
        ...
    elif suffix == \(dqy\(dq:
        ...
    else:
        assert_never(suffix)

if suffix in \(dqdmy\(dq:
    handle_date_suffix(suffix)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Or, instead of \fIassert_never()\fP, you can call a function with a return
annotation of \fINever\fP or \fINoReturn\fP\&. Unlike in the general case, where
by design pylint ignores type annotations and does its own static analysis,
here, pylint treats these special annotations like a disable comment.
.sp
Pylint currently allows repeating the same test like this, even though this
lets some error cases through, as pylint does not assess the intervening code:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
if guarded():
    var = 1

# what if code here affects the result of guarded()?

if guarded():
    print(var)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
But this exception is limited to the repeating the exact same test.
This warns:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
if guarded():
    var = 1

if guarded() or other_condition:
    print(var)  # [possibly\-used\-before\-assignment]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If you find this surprising, consider that pylint, as a static analysis
tool, does not know if \fBguarded()\fP is deterministic or talks to
a database. For variables (e.g. \fBguarded\fP versus \fBguarded()\fP),
this is less of an issue, so in this case,
\fBpossibly\-used\-before\-assignment\fP acts more like a future\-proofing style
preference than an error, per se.
.sp
Created by the variables \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:variables\:.py> checker.
