.SS anomalous\-backslash\-in\-string / W1401
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBAnomalous backslash in string: \(aq%s\(aq. String constant might be missing an r prefix.\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when a backslash is in a literal string but not as an escape.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
string = \(dq\ez\(dq  # [syntax\-error]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.sp
\fBdouble_escape.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
string = \(dq\e\ez\(dq

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBexisting_escape_sequence.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
string = \(dq\et\(dq

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBr_prefix.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
string = r\(dq\ez\(dq

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
\fB\ez\fP is same as \fB\e\ez\fP because there\(aqs no escape sequence for \fBz\fP\&. But it is not clear
for the reader of the code.
.sp
The only reason this is demonstrated to raise \fBsyntax\-error\fP is because
pylint\(aqs CI now runs on Python 3.12, where this truly raises a \fBSyntaxError\fP\&.
We hope to address this discrepancy in the documentation in the future.
.sp
