.SS assert\-on\-string\-literal / W0129
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBAssert statement has a string literal as its first argument. The assert will %s fail.\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when an assert statement has a string literal as its first argument, which will cause the assert to always pass.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def test_division():
    a = 9 / 3
    assert \(dqNo ZeroDivisionError were raised\(dq  # [assert\-on\-string\-literal]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def test_division():
    a = 9 / 3
    assert a == 3

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
Directly asserting a string literal will always pass. The solution is to
test something that could fail, or not assert at all.
.sp
For \fBunittest\fP assertions there is the similar redundant\-unittest\-assert / W1503 \%<#\:redundant-unittest-assert> message.
.sp
\fBRelated links:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Tests without assertion \%<https://\:stackoverflow\:.com/\:a/\:137418/\:2519059>
.IP \(bu 2
Testing that there is no error raised \%<https://\:stackoverflow\:.com/\:questions/\:20274987>
.IP \(bu 2
Parametrizing conditional raising \%<https://\:docs\:.pytest\:.org/\:en/\:latest/\:example/\:parametrize\:.html#\:parametrizing-conditional-raising>
.UNINDENT
