Created by the basic \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:base/\:basic_checker\:.py> checker.
.SS assert\-on\-tuple / W0199
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBAssert called on a populated tuple. Did you mean \(aqassert x,y\(aq?\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIA call of assert on a tuple will always evaluate to true if the tuple is not empty, and will always evaluate to false if it is.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
assert (1, None)  # [assert\-on\-tuple]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
x, y = (1, None)
assert x
assert y

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B Directly asserting a non\-empty tuple will always pass. The solution is to
test something that could fail, or not assert at all.
.sp
For \fBunittest\fP assertions there is the similar redundant\-unittest\-assert / W1503 \%<#\:redundant-unittest-assert> message.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the basic \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:base/\:basic_checker\:.py> checker.
