.SS bare\-except / W0702
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBNo exception type(s) specified\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIA bare \(ga\(gaexcept:\(ga\(ga clause will catch \(ga\(gaSystemExit\(ga\(ga and \(ga\(gaKeyboardInterrupt\(ga\(ga exceptions, making it harder to interrupt a program with \(ga\(gaControl\-C\(ga\(ga, and can disguise other problems. If you want to catch all exceptions that signal program errors, use \(ga\(gaexcept Exception:\(ga\(ga (bare except is equivalent to \(ga\(gaexcept BaseException:\(ga\(ga).\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
try:
    import platform_specific_module
except:  # [bare\-except]
    platform_specific_module = None

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
try:
    import platform_specific_module
except ImportError:
    platform_specific_module = None

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
A good rule of thumb is to limit use of bare ‘except’ clauses to two cases:
\- If the exception handler will be printing out or logging the traceback; at least the user will be aware that an error has occurred.
\- If the code needs to do some cleanup work, but then lets the exception propagate upwards with raise. \fBtry...finally\fP can be a better way to handle this case.
.sp
