\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when a class implements __eq__ but not __hash__. Objects get None as their default __hash__ implementation if they also implement __eq__.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Fruit:  # [eq\-without\-hash]
    def __init__(self) \-> None:
        self.name = \(dqapple\(dq

    def __eq__(self, other: object) \-> bool:
        return isinstance(other, Fruit) and other.name == self.name

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Fruit:
    def __init__(self) \-> None:
        self.name = \(dqapple\(dq

    def __eq__(self, other: object) \-> bool:
        return isinstance(other, Fruit) and other.name == self.name

    def __hash__(self) \-> int:
        return hash(self.name)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBConfiguration file:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[MAIN]
load\-plugins=pylint.extensions.eq_without_hash,

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This message is emitted by the optional \(aqeq\-without\-hash\(aq \%<#\:pylint-extensions-eq-without-hash>
checker, which requires the \fBpylint.extensions.eq_without_hash\fP plugin to be loaded.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the eq\-without\-hash \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:extensions/\:eq_without_hash\:.py> checker.
.SS eval\-used / W0123
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBUse of eval\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when you use the \(dqeval\(dq function, to discourage its usage. Consider using \(gaast.literal_eval\(ga for safely evaluating strings containing Python expressions from untrusted sources.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
eval(\(dq[1, 2, 3]\(dq)  # [eval\-used]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from ast import literal_eval

literal_eval(\(dq[1, 2, 3]\(dq)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the basic \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:base/\:basic_checker\:.py> checker.
.SS exec\-used / W0122
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBUse of exec\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIRaised when the \(aqexec\(aq statement is used. It\(aqs dangerous to use this function for a user input, and it\(aqs also slower than actual code in general. This doesn\(aqt mean you should never use it, but you should consider alternatives first and restrict the functions available.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
username = \(dqAda\(dq
code_to_execute = f\(dq\(dq\(dqinput(\(aqEnter code to be executed please, {username}: \(aq)\(dq\(dq\(dq
program = exec(code_to_execute)  # [exec\-used]
exec(program)  # [exec\-used]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def get_user_code(name):
    return input(f\(dqEnter code to be executed please, {name}: \(dq)


username = \(dqAda\(dq
# If the globals dictionary does not contain a value for the key __builtins__,
# all builtins are allowed. You need to be explicit about it being disallowed.
allowed_globals = {\(dq__builtins__\(dq: {}}
allowed_locals = {}
# pylint: disable\-next=exec\-used
exec(get_user_code(username), allowed_globals, allowed_locals)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
The available methods and variables used in \fBexec()\fP may introduce a security hole.
You can restrict the use of these variables and methods by passing optional globals
and locals parameters (dictionaries) to the \fBexec()\fP method.
.sp
However, use of \fBexec()\fP is still insecure if you allow some functions like
\fB__import__\fP or \fBopen\fP\&. For example, consider the following call that writes a
file to the user\(aqs system and then execute code unrestrained by the \fBallowed_globals\fP,
or \fBallowed_locals\fP parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import textwrap


def forbid_print(*args):
    raise ValueError(\(dqThis is raised when a print is used\(dq)


allowed_globals = {
    \(dq__builtins__\(dq: {
        \(dq__import__\(dq: __builtins__.__import__,
        \(dqopen\(dq: __builtins__.open,
        \(dqprint\(dq: forbid_print,
    }
}

exec(
    textwrap.dedent(
        \(dq\(dq\(dq
    import textwrap

    with open(\(dqnefarious.py\(dq, \(dqw\(dq) as f:
        f.write(textwrap.dedent(\(aq\(aq\(aq
            def connive():
                print(\(dqHere\(aqs some code as nefarious as imaginable\(dq)
        \(aq\(aq\(aq))

    import nefarious

    nefarious.connive()  # This will NOT raise a ValueError
    \(dq\(dq\(dq
    ),
    allowed_globals,
)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The import is used only for readability of the example in this case but it could
import a dangerous functions:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubprocess.run(\(aqecho \(dqprint(\e\(dqHello, World!\e\(dq)\(dq > nefarious.py\(aq\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpathlib.Path(\(dqnefarious.py\(dq).write_file(\(dqprint(\e\(dqHello, World!\e\(dq)\(dq)\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBos.system(\(aqecho \(dqprint(\e\(dqHello, World!\e\(dq)\(dq > nefarious.py\(aq)\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlogging.basicConfig(filename=\(aqnefarious.py\(aq); logging.error(\(aqprint(\(dqHello, World!\(dq)\(aq)\fP
.IP \(bu 2
etc.
.UNINDENT
.sp
