.SS implicit\-str\-concat / W1404
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBImplicit string concatenation found in %s\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIString literals are implicitly concatenated in a literal iterable definition : maybe a comma is missing ?\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.sp
\fBlist.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
x = [\(dqa\(dq \(dqb\(dq]  # [implicit\-str\-concat]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBopen.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
with open(\(dqhello.txt\(dq \(dqr\(dq) as f:  # [implicit\-str\-concat]
    print(f.read())

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.sp
\fBlist.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
x = [\(dqa\(dq, \(dqb\(dq]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBopen.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
with open(\(dqhello.txt\(dq, \(dqr\(dq) as f:
    print(f.read())

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
By default, detection of implicit string concatenation of line jumps is disabled.
Hence the following code will not trigger this rule:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
SEQ = (\(aqa\(aq, \(aqb\(aq
            \(aqc\(aq)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In order to detect this case, you must enable \fIcheck\-str\-concat\-over\-line\-jumps\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[STRING_CONSTANT]
check\-str\-concat\-over\-line\-jumps = true
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
However, the drawback of this setting is that it will trigger false positive
for string parameters passed on multiple lines in function calls:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
warnings.warn(
    \(dqrotate() is deprecated and will be removed in a future release. \(dq
    \(dqUse the rotation() context manager instead.\(dq,
    DeprecationWarning,
    stacklevel=3,
)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
No message will be emitted, though, if you clarify the wanted concatenation with parentheses:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
warnings.warn(
    (
        \(dqrotate() is deprecated and will be removed in a future release. \(dq
        \(dqUse the rotation() context manager instead.\(dq
    ),
    DeprecationWarning,
    stacklevel=3,
)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the string \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:strings\:.py> checker.
.SS import\-self / W0406
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBModule import itself\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when a module is importing itself.\fP
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
Say you have a file called \fBmy_file.py\fP\&. \fBimport\-self\fP would be raised on the following code:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from my_file import a_function  # [import\-self]

def a_function():
    pass
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The solution would be to remove the import:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def a_function():
    pass
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the imports \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:imports\:.py> checker.
.SS inconsistent\-quotes / W1405
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBQuote delimiter %s is inconsistent with the rest of the file\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIQuote delimiters are not used consistently throughout a module (with allowances made for avoiding unnecessary escaping).\fP
