.SS try\-except\-raise / W0706
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBThe except handler raises immediately\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when an except handler uses raise as its first or only operator. This is useless because it raises back the exception immediately. Remove the raise operator or the entire try\-except\-raise block!\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
try:
    1 / 0
except ZeroDivisionError as e:  # [try\-except\-raise]
    raise

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.sp
\fBremove_try_except.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# The try except might be removed entirely:
1 / 0

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBspecialized_exception.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# Another more detailed exception can be raised:
try:
    1 / 0
except ZeroDivisionError as e:
    raise ValueError(\(dqThe area of the rectangle cannot be zero\(dq) from e

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
There is a legitimate use case for re\-raising immediately. E.g. with the following inheritance tree:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
+\-\- ArithmeticError
     +\-\- FloatingPointError
     +\-\- OverflowError
     +\-\- ZeroDivisionError
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following code shows valid case for re\-raising exception immediately:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def execute_calculation(a, b):
    try:
        return some_calculation(a, b)
    except ZeroDivisionError:
        raise
    except ArithmeticError:
        return float(\(aqnan\(aq)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The pylint is able to detect this case and does not produce error.
.sp
Created by the exceptions \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:exceptions\:.py> checker.
