.SS import\-private\-name / C2701
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBImported private %s (%s)\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when a private module or object prefixed with _ is imported. PEP8 guidance on Naming Conventions states that public attributes with leading underscores should be considered private.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from argparse import _AttributeHolder, _SubParsersAction  # [import\-private\-name]

attr_holder = _AttributeHolder()


def add_sub_parser(sub_parsers: _SubParsersAction):
    sub_parsers.add_parser(\(dqmy_subparser\(dq)
    # ...

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dq\(dq\(dqPrivate import can be used as type annotations.\(dq\(dq\(dq

from argparse import _SubParsersAction


def add_sub_parser(sub_parsers: _SubParsersAction):
    sub_parsers.add_parser(\(dqmy_subparser\(dq)
    # ...

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBConfiguration file:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[main]
load\-plugins = pylint.extensions.private_import

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
Using private imports expose you to unexpected breaking changes for any version
bump of your dependencies, even in patch versions.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This message is emitted by the optional \(aqimport\-private\-name\(aq \%<#\:pylint-extensions-private-import>
checker, which requires the \fBpylint.extensions.private_import\fP plugin to be loaded.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the import\-private\-name \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:extensions/\:private_import\:.py> checker.
.SS invalid\-characters\-in\-docstring / C0403
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBInvalid characters %r in a docstring\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when a word in docstring cannot be checked by enchant.\fP
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
This is a message linked to an internal problem in enchant. There\(aqs nothing to change in your code,
but maybe in pylint\(aqs configuration or the way you installed the \(aqenchant\(aq system library.
.sp
Created by the spelling \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:spelling\:.py> checker.
.SS invalid\-name / C0103
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fB%s name \(dq%s\(dq doesn\(aqt conform to %s\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when the name doesn\(aqt conform to naming rules associated to its type (constant, variable, class...).\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class cat:  # [invalid\-name]
    def Meow(self, NUMBER_OF_MEOW):  # [invalid\-name, invalid\-name]
        print(\(dqMeow\(dq * NUMBER_OF_MEOW)
        return NUMBER_OF_MEOW


Cat = cat().Meow(42)  # [invalid\-name]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Cat:
    def meow(self, number_of_meow):
        print(\(dqMeow\(dq * number_of_meow)
        return number_of_meow


CAT = Cat().meow(42)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
Pylint recognizes a number of different name types internally. With a few
exceptions, the type of the name is governed by the location the assignment to a
name is found in, and not the type of object assigned.
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Name Type
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
\fBmodule\fP
T}	T{
Module and package names, same as the file names.
T}
_
T{
\fBconst\fP
T}	T{
Module\-level constants: any name defined at module level that is not bound to a class object nor reassigned.
T}
_
T{
\fBclass\fP
T}	T{
Names in \fBclass\fP statements, as well as names bound to class objects at module level.
T}
_
T{
\fBfunction\fP
T}	T{
Functions, toplevel or nested in functions or methods.
T}
_
T{
\fBmethod\fP
T}	T{
Methods, functions defined in class bodies. Includes static and class methods.
T}
_
T{
\fBattr\fP
T}	T{
Attributes created on class instances inside methods.
T}
_
T{
\fBargument\fP
T}	T{
Arguments to any function type, including lambdas.
T}
_
T{
\fBvariable\fP
T}	T{
Local variables in function scopes or module\-level names that are assigned multiple times.
T}
_
T{
\fBclass\-attribute\fP
T}	T{
Attributes defined in class bodies.
T}
_
T{
\fBclass\-const\fP
T}	T{
Enum constants and class variables annotated with \fBFinal\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBinlinevar\fP
T}	T{
Loop variables in list comprehensions and generator expressions.
T}
_
T{
\fBtypevar\fP
T}	T{
Type variable declared with \fBTypeVar\fP\&.
T}
_
T{
\fBparamspec\fP
T}	T{
Parameter specification variable declared with \fBParamSpec\fP\&.
T}
_
T{
\fBtypevartuple\fP
T}	T{
Type variable tuple declared with \fBTypeVarTuple\fP\&.
T}
_
T{
\fBtypealias\fP
T}	T{
Type alias declared with \fBTypeAlias\fP or assignments of \fBUnion\fP\&.
T}
.TE
.SS Default behavior
.sp
By default, Pylint will enforce PEP8 \%<https://\:peps\:.python\:.org/\:pep-0008>\-suggested names.
.SS Predefined Naming Styles
.sp
Pylint provides set of predefined naming styles. Those predefined
naming styles may be used to adjust Pylint configuration to coding
style used in linted project.
.sp
Following predefined naming styles are available:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsnake_case\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcamelCase\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPascalCase\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUPPER_CASE\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBany\fP \- fake style which does not enforce any limitations
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following options are exposed:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-module\-naming\-style=<style>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-const\-naming\-style=<style>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-class\-naming\-style=<style>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-function\-naming\-style=<style>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-method\-naming\-style=<style>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-attr\-naming\-style=<style>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-argument\-naming\-style=<style>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-variable\-naming\-style=<style>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-class\-attribute\-naming\-style=<style>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-class\-const\-naming\-style=<style>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-inlinevar\-naming\-style=<style>
.UNINDENT
.SS Predefined Naming Patterns
.sp
Pylint provides predefined naming patterns for some names. These patterns are often
based on a Naming Style but there is no option to choose one of the styles mentioned above.
The pattern can be overwritten with the options discussed below.
.sp
The following types of names are checked with a predefined pattern:
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Name type
T}	T{
Good names
T}	T{
Bad names
T}
_
T{
\fBtypevar\fP
T}	T{
\fBT\fP, \fB_CallableT\fP, \fB_T_co\fP, \fBAnyStr\fP,
\fBDeviceTypeT\fP, \fBIPAddressT\fP
T}	T{
\fBDICT_T\fP, \fBCALLABLE_T\fP, \fBENUM_T\fP, \fBDeviceType\fP,
\fB_StrType\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBparamspec\fP
T}	T{
\fBP\fP, \fB_P\fP
T}	T{
\fBCALLABLE_P\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBtypevartuple\fP
T}	T{
\fBTs\fP, \fB_Ts\fP
T}	T{
\fBTUPLE_TS\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBtypealias\fP
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBGoodName\fP, \fB_GoodName\fP, \fBIPAddressType\fP,
\fBGoodName2\fP and other PascalCase variants that
don\(aqt start with \fBT\fP or \fBType\fP\&. This is to
distinguish them from \fBtypevars\fP\&. Note that
\fBTopName\fP is allowed but \fBTTopName\fP isn\(aqt.
.UNINDENT
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBBadNameT\fP, \fBbadName\fP, \fBTBadName\fP, \fBTypeBadName\fP,
\fB_1BadName\fP
.UNINDENT
T}
.TE
.sp
Before pylint 3.0, most predefined patterns also enforced a minimum length
of three characters. If this behavior is desired in versions 3.0 and following,
it can be had by providing custom regular expressions as described next. (Or,
if the \fBdisallowed\-name\fP check is sufficient instead of \fBinvalid\-name\fP,
providing the single option \fBbad\-names\-rgxs=\(dq^..?$\(dq\fP will suffice to fail 1\-2
character names.
.SS Custom regular expressions
.sp
If predefined naming styles are too limited, checker behavior may be further
customized. For each name type, a separate regular expression matching valid
names of this type can be defined. If any of custom regular expressions are
defined, it overrides \fB*\-naming\-style\fP option value.
.sp
Regular expressions for the names are anchored at the beginning, any anchor for
the end must be supplied explicitly. Any name not matching the regular
expression will lead to an instance of \fBinvalid\-name\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-module\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-const\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-class\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-function\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-method\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-attr\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-argument\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-variable\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-class\-attribute\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-class\-const\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-inlinevar\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-typevar\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-paramspec\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-typevartuple\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-typealias\-rgx=<regex>
.UNINDENT
.SS Multiple naming styles for custom regular expressions
.sp
Large code bases that have been worked on for multiple years often exhibit an
evolution in style as well. In some cases, modules can be in the same package,
but still have different naming style based on the stratum they belong to.
However, intra\-module consistency should still be required, to make changes
inside a single file easier. For this case, Pylint supports regular expression
with several named capturing group.
.sp
Rather than emitting name warnings immediately, Pylint will determine the
prevalent naming style inside each module and enforce it on all names.
.sp
Consider the following (simplified) example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
pylint \-\-function\-rgx=\(aq(?:(?P<snake>[a\-z_]+)|(?P<camel>[a\-z]+([A\-Z][a\-z]*)*))$\(aq sample.py
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The regular expression defines two naming styles, \fBsnake\fP for snake\-case
names, and \fBcamel\fP for camel\-case names.
.sp
In \fBsample.py\fP, the function name on line 1 and 7 will mark the module
and enforce the match of named group \fBsnake\fP for the remaining names in
the module:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def valid_snake_case(arg):
   ...

def InvalidCamelCase(arg):
   ...

def more_valid_snake_case(arg):
 ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Because of this, the name on line 4 will trigger an \fBinvalid\-name\fP warning,
even though the name matches the given regex.
.sp
Matches named \fBexempt\fP or \fBignore\fP can be used for non\-tainting names, to
prevent built\-in or interface\-dictated names to trigger certain naming styles.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-name\-group=<name1:name2:...,...>
Default value: empty
.sp
Format: comma\-separated groups of colon\-separated names.
.sp
This option can be used to combine name styles. For example, \fBfunction:method\fP enforces that functions and methods use the same style, and a style triggered by either name type carries over to the other. This requires that the regular expression for the combined name types use the same group names.
.UNINDENT
.SS Name Hints
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-include\-naming\-hint=y|n
Default: off
.sp
Include a hint (regular expression used) for the correct name format with every \fBinvalid\-name\fP warning.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the basic \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:base/\:name_checker/\:checker\:.py> checker.
.SS line\-too\-long / C0301
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBLine too long (%s/%s)\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when a line is longer than a given number of characters.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# +1: [line\-too\-long]
FRUIT = [\(dqapricot\(dq, \(dqblackcurrant\(dq, \(dqcantaloupe\(dq, \(dqdragon fruit\(dq, \(dqelderberry\(dq, \(dqfig\(dq, \(dqgrapefruit\(dq, ]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
FRUIT = [
    \(dqapricot\(dq,
    \(dqblackcurrant\(dq,
    \(dqcantaloupe\(dq,
    \(dqdragon fruit\(dq,
    \(dqelderberry\(dq,
    \(dqfig\(dq,
    \(dqgrapefruit\(dq,
]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBConfiguration file:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[MAIN]
max\-line\-length=100

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
Pragma controls such as \fB# pylint: disable=all\fP are not counted toward line length for the purposes of this message.
.sp
If you attempt to disable this message via \fB# pylint: disable=line\-too\-long\fP in a module with no code, you may receive a message for \fBuseless\-suppression\fP\&. This is a false positive of \fBuseless\-suppression\fP we can\(aqt easily fix.
.sp
See \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:issues/\:3368> for more information.
.sp
Created by the format \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:format\:.py> checker.
.SS misplaced\-comparison\-constant / C2201
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBComparison should be %s\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when the constant is placed on the left side of a comparison. It is usually clearer in intent to place it in the right hand side of the comparison.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def compare_apples(apples=20):
    for i in range(10):
        if 5 <= i:  # [misplaced\-comparison\-constant]
            pass
        if 1 == i:  # [misplaced\-comparison\-constant]
            pass
        if 20 < len(apples):  # [misplaced\-comparison\-constant]
            pass

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def compare_apples(apples=20):
    for i in range(10):
        if i >= 5:
            pass
        if i == 1:
            pass
        if len(apples) > 20:
            pass

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBConfiguration file:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[MAIN]
load\-plugins=pylint.extensions.comparison_placement

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This message is emitted by the optional \(aqcomparison\-placement\(aq \%<#\:pylint-extensions-comparison-placement>
checker, which requires the \fBpylint.extensions.comparison_placement\fP plugin to be loaded.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the comparison\-placement \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:extensions/\:comparison_placement\:.py> checker.
.SS missing\-class\-docstring / C0115
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBMissing class docstring\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when a class has no docstring. Even an empty class must have a docstring.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Person:  # [missing\-class\-docstring]
    def __init__(self, first_name, last_name):
        self.first_name = first_name
        self.last_name = last_name

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Person:
    \(dq\(dq\(dqClass representing a person\(dq\(dq\(dq

    def __init__(self, first_name, last_name):
        self.first_name = first_name
        self.last_name = last_name

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the basic \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:base/\:docstring_checker\:.py> checker.
.SS missing\-final\-newline / C0304
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBFinal newline missing\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when the last line in a file is missing a newline.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.sp
\fBcrlf.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
print(\(dqHello\(dq)  # CRLF (\er\en)
print(\(dqworld\(dq)  # End\-of\-file (EOF)
# [missing\-final\-newline]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBlf.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
print(\(dqHello\(dq)  # LF (\en)
print(\(dqworld\(dq)  # End\-of\-file (EOF)
# [missing\-final\-newline]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.sp
\fBcrlf.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
print(\(dqHello\(dq)  # CRLF (\er\en)
print(\(dqworld\(dq)  # CRLF (\er\en)
# End\-of\-file (EOF)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBlf.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
print(\(dqHello\(dq)  # LF (\en)
print(\(dqworld\(dq)  # LF (\en)
# End\-of\-file (EOF)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B The POSIX standard defines a line as:
\(dqA sequence of zero or more non\- <newline> characters plus a terminating <newline> character.\(dq
.UNINDENT
.sp
