.SS confusing\-consecutive\-elif / R5601
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBConsecutive elif with differing indentation level, consider creating a function to separate the inner elif\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when an elif statement follows right after an indented block which itself ends with if or elif. It may not be obvious if the elif statement was willingly or mistakenly unindented. Extracting the indented if statement into a separate function might avoid confusion and prevent errors.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def myfunc(shall_continue: bool, shall_exit: bool):
    if shall_continue:
        if input(\(dqAre you sure?\(dq) == \(dqy\(dq:
            print(\(dqMoving on.\(dq)
    elif shall_exit:  # [confusing\-consecutive\-elif]
        print(\(dqExiting.\(dq)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# Option 1: add explicit \(aqelse\(aq
def myfunc(shall_continue: bool, shall_exit: bool):
    if shall_continue:
        if input(\(dqAre you sure?\(dq) == \(dqy\(dq:
            print(\(dqMoving on.\(dq)
        else:
            pass
    elif shall_exit:
        print(\(dqExiting.\(dq)


# Option 2: extract function
def user_confirmation():
    if input(\(dqAre you sure?\(dq) == \(dqy\(dq:
        print(\(dqMoving on.\(dq)


def myfunc2(shall_continue: bool, shall_exit: bool):
    if shall_continue:
        user_confirmation()
    elif shall_exit:
        print(\(dqExiting.\(dq)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBConfiguration file:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[MAIN]
load\-plugins=pylint.extensions.confusing_elif

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
Creating a function for the nested conditional, or adding an explicit \fBelse\fP in the indented \fBif\fP statement, even if it only contains a \fBpass\fP statement, can help clarify the code.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
