.SS consider\-alternative\-union\-syntax / R6003
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBConsider using alternative union syntax instead of \(aq%s\(aq%s\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIEmitted when \(ga\(gatyping.Union\(ga\(ga or \(ga\(gatyping.Optional\(ga\(ga is used instead of the shorthand union syntax. For example, \(ga\(gaUnion[int, float]\(ga\(ga instead of \(ga\(gaint | float\(ga\(ga. Using the shorthand for unions aligns with Python typing recommendations, removes the need for imports, and avoids confusion in function signatures.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from typing import Optional, Union


def forecast(
    temp: Union[int, float],  # [consider\-alternative\-union\-syntax]
    unit: Optional[str],  # [consider\-alternative\-union\-syntax]
) \-> None:
    print(f\(aqTemperature: {temp}{unit or \(dq\(dq}\(aq)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def forecast(temp: int | float, unit: str | None) \-> None:
    print(f\(aqTemperature: {temp}{unit or \(dq\(dq}\(aq)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBConfiguration file:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[MAIN]
load\-plugins = pylint.extensions.typing

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
Using the shorthand syntax for union types is recommended over the \fBtyping\fP module \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:library/\:typing\:.html#\:typing\:.Union>\&. This is consistent with the broader recommendation to prefer built\-in types over imports (for example, using \fBlist\fP instead of the now\-deprecated \fBtyping.List\fP).
.sp
\fBtyping.Optional\fP can also cause confusion in annotated function arguments, since an argument annotated as \fBOptional\fP is still a \fIrequired\fP argument when a default value is not set. Explicitly annotating such arguments with \fBtype | None\fP makes the intention clear.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This message is emitted by the optional \(aqtyping\(aq \%<#\:pylint-extensions-typing>
checker, which requires the \fBpylint.extensions.typing\fP plugin to be loaded.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the typing \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:extensions/\:typing\:.py> checker.
.SS consider\-math\-not\-float / R6106
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBConsider %smath.%s instead of %s\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsing math.inf or math.nan permits to benefit from typing and it is up to 4 times faster than a float call (after the initial import of math). This check also catches typos in float calls as a side effect.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
swag = float(\(dqinf\(dq)  # [consider\-math\-not\-float]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import math

swag = math.inf

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBConfiguration file:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[MAIN]
load\-plugins=pylint.extensions.code_style

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
This is an extension check because the typing advantage could be fixed.
.sp
Regarding performance, float(\(dqnan\(dq) and float(\(dqinf\(dq) are slower than their counterpart math.inf and math.nan by a factor of 4 after the initial import of math.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import math
import timeit

time_math_inf = timeit.timeit(\(aqmath.nan\(aq, globals=globals(), number=10**8)
print(f\(aqmath.nan: {time_math_inf:.2f} seconds\(aq)

import timeit
time_inf_str = timeit.timeit(\(aqfloat(\(dqnan\(dq)\(aq, number=10**8)
print(f\(aqfloat(\(dqnan\(dq): {time_inf_str:.2f} seconds\(aq)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
math.nan: 1.24 seconds
float(\(dqnan\(dq): 5.15 seconds
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
But if we take the initial import into account it\(aqs worse.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import timeit

time_math_inf = timeit.timeit(\(aqimport math;math.nan\(aq, globals=globals(), number=10**8)
print(f\(aqmath.nan: {time_math_inf:.2f} seconds\(aq)

import timeit
time_inf_str = timeit.timeit(\(aqfloat(\(dqnan\(dq)\(aq, number=10**8)
print(f\(aqfloat(\(dqnan\(dq): {time_inf_str:.2f} seconds\(aq)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
math.nan: 9.08 seconds
float(\(dqnan\(dq): 5.33 seconds
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
So the decision depends on how and how often you need to use it and what matter to you.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This message is emitted by the optional \(aqcode_style\(aq \%<#\:pylint-extensions-code-style>
checker, which requires the \fBpylint.extensions.code_style\fP plugin to be loaded.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the code_style \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:extensions/\:code_style\:.py> checker.
