.SS consider\-using\-with / R1732
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBConsider using \(aqwith\(aq for resource\-allocating operations\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIEmitted if a resource\-allocating assignment or call may be replaced by a \(aqwith\(aq block. By using \(aqwith\(aq the release of the allocated resources is ensured even in the case of an exception.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.sp
\fBclose.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
file = open(\(dqapple.txt\(dq, \(dqr\(dq, encoding=\(dqutf8\(dq)  # [consider\-using\-with]
contents = file.read()
file.close()

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBnot_even_close.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
contents = open(\(dqapple.txt\(dq, \(dqr\(dq, encoding=\(dqutf8\(dq).read()  # [consider\-using\-with]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
with open(\(dqapple.txt\(dq, \(dqr\(dq, encoding=\(dqutf8\(dq) as file:
    contents = file.read()

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
Calling \fBwrite()\fP without using the \fBwith\fP keyword or calling \fBclose()\fP might
result in the arguments of \fBwrite()\fP not being completely written to the disk,
even if the program exits successfully.
.sp
This message applies to callables of Python\(aqs stdlib which can be replaced by a \fBwith\fP statement.
It is suppressed in the following cases:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
the call is located inside a context manager
.IP \(bu 2
the call result is returned from the enclosing function
.IP \(bu 2
the call result is used in a \fBwith\fP statement itself
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBRelated links:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Python doc: Reading and writing files \%<https://\:docs\:.python\:.org/\:3/\:tutorial/\:inputoutput\:.html#\:reading-and-writing-files>
.IP \(bu 2
PEP 343 \%<https://\:peps\:.python\:.org/\:pep-0343>
.IP \(bu 2
Context managers in Python \%<https://\:johnlekberg\:.com/\:blog/\:2020-10-11-ctx-manage\:.html> by John Lekberg
.IP \(bu 2
Rationale \%<https://\:stackoverflow\:.com/\:a/\:73181877/\:2519059>
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the refactoring \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:refactoring/\:refactoring_checker\:.py> checker.
.SS cyclic\-import / R0401
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBCyclic import (%s)\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when a cyclic import between two or more modules is detected.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.sp
\fB__init__.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBbad.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def count_to_one():
    return 1


def count_to_three():
    from .bad2 import count_to_two

    return count_to_two() + 1

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBbad2.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from .bad import count_to_one  # [cyclic\-import]


def count_to_two():
    return count_to_one() + 1

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def count_to_one():
    return 1


def count_to_two():
    return count_to_one() + 1


def count_to_three():
    return count_to_two() + 1

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
The good code is just an example. There are various strategies to resolving
cyclic imports and the best choice relies heavily on the context of the code
and the affected modules.
.sp
Created by the imports \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:imports\:.py> checker.
.SS duplicate\-code / R0801
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBSimilar lines in %s files
%s\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIIndicates that a set of similar lines has been detected among multiple file. This usually means that the code should be refactored to avoid this duplication.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.sp
\fB__init__.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBapple.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Apple:
    def __init__(self):
        self.remaining_bites = 3

    def take_bite(self):
        if self.remaining_bites > 0:
            print(\(dqYou take a bite of the apple.\(dq)
            self.remaining_bites \-= 1
        else:
            print(\(dqThe apple is already eaten up!\(dq)

    def eaten_by_animal(self, animal):
        self.remaining_bites = 0
        print(\(dqThe apple has been eaten by an animal.\(dq)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBorange.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Orange:  # [duplicate\-code]
    def __init__(self):
        self.remaining_bites = 3

    def take_bite(self):
        if self.remaining_bites > 0:
            print(\(dqYou take a bite of the apple.\(dq)
            self.remaining_bites \-= 1
        else:
            print(\(dqThe orange is already eaten up!\(dq)

    def eaten_by_animal(self, animal):
        if animal == \(dqcat\(dq:
            raise ValueError(\(dqA cat would never do that !\(dq)
        self.remaining_bites = 0
        print(\(dqThe orange has been eaten by an animal.\(dq)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.sp
\fB__init__.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBapple.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from fruit import Fruit


class Apple(Fruit): ...

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBfruit.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Fruit:
    def __init__(self):
        self.remaining_bites = 3

    def take_bite(self):
        if self.remaining_bites > 0:
            print(f\(dqYou take a bite of the {self.__class__.__name__.lower()}.\(dq)
            self.remaining_bites \-= 1
        else:
            print(f\(dqThe {self.__class__.__name__.lower()} is already eaten up!\(dq)

    def eaten_by_animal(self, animal):
        self.remaining_bites = 0
        print(f\(dqThe {self.__class__.__name__.lower()} has been eaten by an animal.\(dq)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBorange.py\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from fruit import Fruit


class Orange(Fruit):
    def eaten_by_animal(self, animal):
        if animal == \(dqcat\(dq:
            raise ValueError(\(dqA cat would never do that !\(dq)
        super().eaten_by_animal(animal)

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
If you need to make a change to the logic or functionality of the duplicated
code, you will need to identify all the places that need to be changed, which
can be time\-consuming and error\-prone. If there are multiple copies of the
same code, then you will also need to test each copy to ensure that the
functionality is correct. Duplicate code can be confusing for someone who is
trying to understand the logic and flow of the code if they come across multiple
identical or nearly identical blocks of code. The reader can then skim and
think something is identical when it actually isn\(aqt. This is particularly true
during review.
.sp
Created by the similarities \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:symilar\:.py> checker.
.SS else\-if\-used / R5501
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBConsider using \(dqelif\(dq instead of \(dqelse\(dq then \(dqif\(dq to remove one indentation level\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when an else statement is immediately followed by an if statement and does not contain statements that would be unrelated to it.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
if input():
    pass
else:
    if len(input()) >= 10:  # [else\-if\-used]
        pass
    else:
        pass

.EE
