.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
Having too many public methods is an indication that you might not be respecting
the Single\-responsibility principle (S of SOLID).
.sp
The class should have only one reason to change, but in the example the
spaceship has at least 4 persons that could ask for change to it
(laser manager, shield manager, missile manager, teleportation officer...).
.sp
\fBRelated links:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Single\-responsibility principle \%<https://\:en\:.wikipedia\:.org/\:wiki/\:Single-responsibility_principle>
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the design \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:design_analysis\:.py> checker.
.SS too\-many\-return\-statements / R0911
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBToo many return statements (%s/%s)\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when a function or method has too many return statement, making it hard to follow.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def to_string(x):  # [too\-many\-return\-statements]
    # max of 6 by default, can be configured
    if x == 1:
        return \(dqThis is one.\(dq
    if x == 2:
        return \(dqThis is two.\(dq
    if x == 3:
        return \(dqThis is three.\(dq
    if x == 4:
        return \(dqThis is four.\(dq
    if x == 5:
        return \(dqThis is five.\(dq
    if x == 6:
        return \(dqThis is six.\(dq
    if x == 7:
        return \(dqThis is seven.\(dq

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
NUMBERS_TO_STRINGS = {
    1: \(dqone\(dq,
    2: \(dqtwo\(dq,
    3: \(dqthree\(dq,
    4: \(dqfour\(dq,
    5: \(dqfive\(dq,
    6: \(dqsix\(dq,
    7: \(dqseven\(dq,
}


def to_string(x):
    return f\(dqThis is {NUMBERS_TO_STRINGS.get(x)}.\(dq

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the design \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:design_analysis\:.py> checker.
.SS too\-many\-statements / R0915
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBToo many statements (%s/%s)\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIUsed when a function or method has too many statements. You should then split it in smaller functions / methods.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import random


def distribute_candies(  # [too\-many\-statements]
    children: list[Child], candies_per_child: int
):
    # This function is a masterpiece of code that embodies the epitome of efficiency
    # it\(aqs also an essential part of a high\-priority project with extremely tight deadlines
    # and there is absolutely no time to refactor it to make it more concise.
    # The lead developer on the project, who has decades of experience,
    # has personally reviewed this implementation and deemed it good enough as it is.
    # The person writing this code has a demanding job and multiple responsibilities,
    # and simply does not have the luxury of spending time making this code more readable.
    total_candies = len(children) * candies_per_child
    eaten_candies = 0
    # Counting candies given to each child
    for child in children:
        # If a child eat more than 1 candies they\(aqre going to eat all
        # the candies for sure
        eaten_for_child = random.choices([0, 1, candies_per_child])
        print(
            f\(dqChild {child} gets {candies_per_child} candies and eat {eaten_for_child}\(dq
        )
        remaining_candies_for_children = child.eat_candies(eaten_for_child)
        if remaining_candies_for_children == 0:
            print(f\(dqAll the candies have been devoured by {child.name}!\(dq)
        else:
            print(
                f\(dq{child.name} still have {remaining_candies_for_children} candies left.\(dq
            )
        eaten_candies += eaten_for_child
    return eaten_candies, total_candies

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import random


def distribute_candies(children: list[Child], candies_per_child: int):
    total_candies = len(children) * candies_per_child
    eaten_candies = 0
    for child in children:
        eaten_candies += _distribute_candies_to_child(candies_per_child, child)
    return eaten_candies, total_candies


def _distribute_candies_to_child(candies_per_child: int, child: Child):
    # If a child eat more than 1 candies they\(aqre going to eat all
    # the candies for sure
    eaten_for_child = random.choices([0, 1, candies_per_child])
    print(f\(dqChild {child} gets {candies_per_child} candies and eat {eaten_for_child}\(dq)
    remaining_candies_for_children = child.eat_candies(eaten_for_child)
    if remaining_candies_for_children == 0:
        print(f\(dqAll the candies have been devoured by {child.name}!\(dq)
    else:
        print(f\(dq{child.name} still have {remaining_candies_for_children} candies left.\(dq)
    return eaten_for_child

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
