.sp
Created by the refactoring \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:checkers/\:refactoring/\:refactoring_checker\:.py> checker.
.SS use\-a\-generator / R1729
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBUse a generator instead \(aq%s(%s)\(aq\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIComprehension inside of \(aqany\(aq, \(aqall\(aq, \(aqmax\(aq, \(aqmin\(aq or \(aqsum\(aq is unnecessary. A generator would be sufficient and faster.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from random import randint

all([randint(\-5, 5) > 0 for _ in range(10)])  # [use\-a\-generator]
any([randint(\-5, 5) > 0 for _ in range(10)])  # [use\-a\-generator]

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from random import randint

all(randint(\-5, 5) > 0 for _ in range(10))
any(randint(\-5, 5) > 0 for _ in range(10))

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
By using a generator you can cut the execution tree and exit directly at the first element that is \fBFalse\fP for \fBall\fP or \fBTrue\fP for \fBany\fP instead of
calculating all the elements. Except in the worst possible case where you still need to evaluate everything (all values
are True for \fBall\fP or all values are false for \fBany\fP) performance will be better.
.sp
\fBRelated links:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
PEP 289 – Generator Expressions \%<https://\:peps\:.python\:.org/\:pep-0289>
.IP \(bu 2
Benchmark and discussion during initial implementation \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:pull/\:3309#\:discussion_r576683109>
.UNINDENT
.sp
