.SS use\-yield\-from / R1737
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBUse \(aqyield from\(aq directly instead of yielding each element one by one\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIYielding directly from the iterator is faster and arguably cleaner code than yielding each element one by one in the loop.\fP
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def bad_yield_from(generator):
    for item in generator:  # [use\-yield\-from]
        yield item

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCorrect code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def good_yield_from(generator):
    yield from generator

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAdditional details:\fP
.sp
\fByield from\fP can be thought of as removing the intermediary (your for loop) between the function caller and the
requested generator. This enables the caller to directly communicate with the generator (e.g. using \fBsend()\fP).
This communication is not possible when manually yielding each element one by one in a loop.
.sp
PEP 380 describes the possibility of adding optimizations specific to \fByield from\fP\&. It looks like they
have not been implemented as of the time of writing. Even without said optimizations, the following snippet shows
that \fByield from\fP is marginally faster.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ python3 \-m timeit \(dqdef yield_from(): yield from range(100)\(dq \(dqfor _ in yield_from(): pass\(dq
100000 loops, best of 5: 2.44 usec per loop
$ python3 \-m timeit \(dqdef yield_loop():\(dq \(dq    for item in range(100): yield item\(dq \(dqfor _ in yield_loop(): pass\(dq
100000 loops, best of 5: 2.49 usec per loop
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBRelated links:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
PEP 380 \%<https://\:peps\:.python\:.org/\:pep-0380>
