.SS suppressed\-message / I0020
.sp
\fBMessage emitted:\fP
.sp
\fBSuppressed %s (from line %d)\fP
.sp
\fBDescription:\fP
.sp
\fIA message was triggered on a line, but suppressed explicitly by a disable= comment in the file. This message is not generated for messages that are ignored due to configuration settings.\fP
.sp
\fBCaution:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This message is disabled by default. To enable it, add \fBsuppressed\-message\fP to the \fBenable\fP option.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCaution:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
By default, this message will not fail the execution (pylint will return 0).
To make pylint fail for this message use the \fB\-\-fail\-on=I0020\fP option
or \fB\-\-fail\-on=I\fP to fail on all enabled informational messages.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBProblematic code:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
### This is a contrived example, to show how suppressed\-message works.
### First we enable all messages
# pylint: enable=all

### Here we disable two messages so we get two warnings
# pylint: disable=locally\-disabled, useless\-suppression # [suppressed\-message, suppressed\-message]

### Here we disable a message, so we get a warning for suppressed\-message again.
\(dqA\(dq  # pylint: disable=pointless\-statement # [suppressed\-message, suppressed\-message]

.EE
