.UNINDENT
.sp
Created by the main \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pylint-dev/\:pylint/\:blob/\:main/\:build/\:lib/\:pylint/\:lint/\:pylinter\:.py> checker.
.sp
All renamed messages in the information category:
.SS deprecated\-disable\-all / I0014
.sp
\(aqdeprecated\-disable\-all\(aq has been renamed. The new message can be found at:
.SS Messages control
.sp
In order to control messages, \fBpylint\fP accepts the following values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
a symbolic message: \fBno\-member\fP, \fBundefined\-variable\fP etc.
.IP \(bu 2
a numerical ID: \fBE1101\fP, \fBE1102\fP etc.
.IP \(bu 2
The name of the group of checks. You can grab those with \fBpylint \-\-list\-groups\fP\&.
For example, you can disable / enable all the checks related to type checking, with
\fBtypecheck\fP or all the checks related to variables with \fBvariables\fP
.IP \(bu 2
Corresponding category of the checks
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBC\fP convention related checks
.IP \(bu 2
\fBR\fP refactoring related checks
.IP \(bu 2
\fBW\fP various warnings
.IP \(bu 2
\fBE\fP errors, for probable bugs in the code
.IP \(bu 2
\fBF\fP fatal, if an error occurred which prevented \fBpylint\fP from doing further processing.
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
All the checks with \fBall\fP
.UNINDENT
.SS Block disables
.sp
This describes how the pragma controls operate at a code level.
.sp
The pragma controls can disable / enable:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
All the violations on a single line
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
a, b = ... # pylint: disable=unbalanced\-tuple\-unpacking
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
All the violations on the following line
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# pylint: disable\-next=unbalanced\-tuple\-unpacking
a, b = ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
All the violations in a single scope
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def test():
    # Disable all the no\-member violations in this function
    # pylint: disable=no\-member
    ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
All the violations in a \fIblock\fP\&. For instance, each separate branch of an
\fBif\fP statement is considered a separate block, as in the following example:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
def meth5(self):
    # pylint: disable=no\-member
    # no error
    print(self.bla)
    if self.blop:
        # pylint: enable=no\-member
        # enable all no\-members for this block
        print(self.blip)
    else:
        # This is affected by the scope disable
        print(self.blip)
    # pylint: enable=no\-member
    print(self.blip)
    if self.blop:
        # pylint: disable=no\-member
        # disable all no\-members for this block
        print(self.blip)
    else:
        # This emits a violation
        print(self.blip)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
If the violation occurs on a block starting line, then it applies only to that line
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
if self.blop: # pylint: disable=no\-member; applies only to this line
    # Here we get an error
    print(self.blip)
else:
    # error
    print(self.blip)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here\(aqs an example with all these rules in a single place:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dq\(dq\(dqpylint option block\-disable\(dq\(dq\(dq

__revision__ = None

class Foo(object):
    \(dq\(dq\(dqblock\-disable test\(dq\(dq\(dq

    def __init__(self):
        pass

    def meth1(self, arg):
        \(dq\(dq\(dqthis issues a message\(dq\(dq\(dq
        print(self)

    def meth2(self, arg):
        \(dq\(dq\(dqand this one not\(dq\(dq\(dq
        # pylint: disable=unused\-argument
        print(self\e
              + \(dqfoo\(dq)

    def meth3(self):
        \(dq\(dq\(dqtest one line disabling\(dq\(dq\(dq
        # no error
        print(self.bla) # pylint: disable=no\-member
        # error
        print(self.blop)

    def meth4(self):
        \(dq\(dq\(dqtest re\-enabling\(dq\(dq\(dq
        # pylint: disable=no\-member
        # no error
        print(self.bla)
        print(self.blop)
        # pylint: enable=no\-member
        # error
        print(self.blip)

    def meth5(self):
        \(dq\(dq\(dqtest IF sub\-block re\-enabling\(dq\(dq\(dq
        # pylint: disable=no\-member
        # no error
        print(self.bla)
        if self.blop:
            # pylint: enable=no\-member
            # error
            print(self.blip)
        else:
            # no error
            print(self.blip)
        # no error
        print(self.blip)

    def meth6(self):
        \(dq\(dq\(dqtest TRY/EXCEPT sub\-block re\-enabling\(dq\(dq\(dq
        # pylint: disable=no\-member
        # no error
        print(self.bla)
        try:
            # pylint: enable=no\-member
            # error
            print(self.blip)
        except UndefinedName: # pylint: disable=undefined\-variable
            # no error
            print(self.blip)
        # no error
        print(self.blip)

    def meth7(self):
        \(dq\(dq\(dqtest one line block opening disabling\(dq\(dq\(dq
        if self.blop: # pylint: disable=no\-member
            # error
            print(self.blip)
        else:
            # error
            print(self.blip)
        # error
        print(self.blip)

    def meth8(self):
        \(dq\(dq\(dqtest late disabling\(dq\(dq\(dq
        # error
        print(self.blip)
        # pylint: disable=no\-member
        # no error
        print(self.bla)
        print(self.blop)

    def meth9(self):
        \(dq\(dq\(dqtest next line disabling\(dq\(dq\(dq
        # no error
        # pylint: disable\-next=no\-member
        print(self.bla)
        # error
        print(self.blop)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Detecting useless disables
.sp
As pylint gets better and false positives are removed,
disables that became useless can accumulate and clutter the code.
In order to clean them you can enable the \fBuseless\-suppression\fP warning.
.SS Messages categories
.sp
Pylint can emit various messages. These are categorized according
to categories corresponding to bit\-encoded exit codes:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Fatal \%<#\:fatal-category> (1)
.IP \(bu 2
Error \%<#\:error-category> (2)
.IP \(bu 2
Warning \%<#\:warning-category> (4)
.IP \(bu 2
Convention \%<#\:convention-category> (8)
.IP \(bu 2
Refactor \%<#\:refactor-category> (16)
.IP \(bu 2
Information \%<#\:information-category> (NA)
.UNINDENT
.sp
An overview of these messages can be found in Messages overview \%<#\:messages-overview>
.SS Disabling messages
.sp
\fBpylint\fP has an advanced message control for its checks, offering the ability
to enable / disable a message either from the command line or from the configuration
file, as well as from the code itself.
.sp
For more detail see Messages control \%<#\:message-control>
.SS Configuration
.sp
Pylint is highly configurable. There are a lot of options to follow the needs of
various projects and a lot of checks to activate if they suit your style.
.sp
You can generate a sample configuration file with \fB\-\-generate\-toml\-config\fP
or \fB\-\-generate\-rcfile\fP\&. Every option present on the command line before this
will be included in the file.
.sp
For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
pylint \-\-disable=bare\-except,invalid\-name \-\-class\-rgx=\(aq[A\-Z][a\-z]+\(aq \-\-generate\-toml\-config
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In practice, it is often better to create a minimal configuration file which only contains
configuration overrides. For all other options, Pylint will use its default values.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The internals that create the configuration files fall back to the default values if
no other value was given. This means that some values depend on the interpreter that
was used to generate the file. Most notably \fBpy\-version\fP which defaults to the
current interpreter.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Standard Checkers
.SS \fBMain\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-analyse\-fallback\-blocks
.sp
\fIAnalyse import fallback blocks. This can be used to support both Python 2 and 3 compatible code, which means that the block might have code that exists only in one or another interpreter, leading to false positives when analysed.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-clear\-cache\-post\-run
.sp
\fIClear in\-memory caches upon conclusion of linting. Useful if running pylint in a server\-like mode.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-confidence
.sp
\fIOnly show warnings with the listed confidence levels. Leave empty to show all. Valid levels: HIGH, CONTROL_FLOW, INFERENCE, INFERENCE_FAILURE, UNDEFINED.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB[\(aqHIGH\(aq, \(aqCONTROL_FLOW\(aq, \(aqINFERENCE\(aq, \(aqINFERENCE_FAILURE\(aq, \(aqUNDEFINED\(aq]\fP
.SS \-\-disable
.sp
\fIDisable the message, report, category or checker with the given id(s). You can either give multiple identifiers separated by comma (,) or put this option multiple times (only on the command line, not in the configuration file where it should appear only once). You can also use \(dq\-\-disable=all\(dq to disable everything first and then re\-enable specific checks. For example, if you want to run only the similarities checker, you can use \(dq\-\-disable=all \-\-enable=similarities\(dq. If you want to run only the classes checker, but have no Warning level messages displayed, use \(dq\-\-disable=all \-\-enable=classes \-\-disable=W\(dq.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-enable
.sp
\fIEnable the message, report, category or checker with the given id(s). You can either give multiple identifier separated by comma (,) or put this option multiple time (only on the command line, not in the configuration file where it should appear only once). See also the \(dq\-\-disable\(dq option for examples.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-evaluation
.sp
\fIPython expression which should return a score less than or equal to 10. You have access to the variables \(aqfatal\(aq, \(aqerror\(aq, \(aqwarning\(aq, \(aqrefactor\(aq, \(aqconvention\(aq, and \(aqinfo\(aq which contain the number of messages in each category, as well as \(aqstatement\(aq which is the total number of statements analyzed. This score is used by the global evaluation report (RP0004).\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBmax(0, 0 if fatal else 10.0 \- ((float(5 * error + warning + refactor + convention) / statement) * 10))\fP
.SS \-\-exit\-zero
.sp
\fIAlways return a 0 (non\-error) status code, even if lint errors are found. This is primarily useful in continuous integration scripts.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-extension\-pkg\-allow\-list
.sp
\fIA comma\-separated list of package or module names from where C extensions may be loaded. Extensions are loading into the active Python interpreter and may run arbitrary code.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB[]\fP
.SS \-\-extension\-pkg\-whitelist
.sp
\fIA comma\-separated list of package or module names from where C extensions may be loaded. Extensions are loading into the active Python interpreter and may run arbitrary code. (This is an alternative name to extension\-pkg\-allow\-list for backward compatibility.)\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB[]\fP
.SS \-\-fail\-on
.sp
\fIReturn non\-zero exit code if any of these messages/categories are detected, even if score is above \-\-fail\-under value. Syntax same as enable. Messages specified are enabled, while categories only check already\-enabled messages.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.SS \-\-fail\-under
.sp
\fISpecify a score threshold under which the program will exit with error.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB10\fP
.SS \-\-from\-stdin
.sp
\fIInterpret the stdin as a python script, whose filename needs to be passed as the module_or_package argument.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-ignore
.sp
\fIFiles or directories to be skipped. They should be base names, not paths.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqCVS\(aq,)\fP
.SS \-\-ignore\-paths
.sp
\fIAdd files or directories matching the regular expressions patterns to the ignore\-list. The regex matches against paths and can be in Posix or Windows format. Because \(aq\e\(aq represents the directory delimiter on Windows systems, it can\(aqt be used as an escape character.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB[]\fP
.SS \-\-ignore\-patterns
.sp
\fIFiles or directories matching the regular expression patterns are skipped. The regex matches against base names, not paths. The default value ignores Emacs file locks\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(re.compile(\(aq^\e\e.#\(aq),)\fP
.SS \-\-ignored\-modules
.sp
\fIList of module names for which member attributes should not be checked and will not be imported (useful for modules/projects where namespaces are manipulated during runtime and thus existing member attributes cannot be deduced by static analysis). It supports qualified module names, as well as Unix pattern matching.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-jobs
.sp
\fIUse multiple processes to speed up Pylint. Specifying 0 will auto\-detect the number of processors available to use, and will cap the count on Windows to avoid hangs.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB1\fP
.SS \-\-limit\-inference\-results
.sp
\fIControl the amount of potential inferred values when inferring a single object. This can help the performance when dealing with large functions or complex, nested conditions.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB100\fP
.SS \-\-load\-plugins
.sp
\fIList of plugins (as comma separated values of python module names) to load, usually to register additional checkers.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-msg\-template
.sp
\fITemplate used to display messages. This is a python new\-style format string used to format the message information. See doc for all details.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.SS \-\-output\-format
.sp
\fISet the output format. Available formats are: \(aqtext\(aq, \(aqparseable\(aq, \(aqcolorized\(aq, \(aqjson2\(aq (improved json format), \(aqjson\(aq (old json format), msvs (visual studio) and \(aqgithub\(aq (GitHub actions). You can also give a reporter class, e.g. mypackage.mymodule.MyReporterClass.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBtext\fP
.SS \-\-persistent
.sp
\fIPickle collected data for later comparisons.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-prefer\-stubs
.sp
\fIResolve imports to .pyi stubs if available. May reduce no\-member messages and increase not\-an\-iterable messages.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-py\-version
.sp
\fIMinimum Python version to use for version dependent checks. Will default to the version used to run pylint.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBsys.version_info[:2]\fP
.SS \-\-recursive
.sp
\fIDiscover python modules and packages in the file system subtree.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-reports
.sp
\fITells whether to display a full report or only the messages.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-score
.sp
\fIActivate the evaluation score.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-source\-roots
.sp
\fIAdd paths to the list of the source roots. Supports globbing patterns. The source root is an absolute path or a path relative to the current working directory used to determine a package namespace for modules located under the source root.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-unsafe\-load\-any\-extension
.sp
\fIAllow loading of arbitrary C extensions. Extensions are imported into the active Python interpreter and may run arbitrary code.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.main]
analyse\-fallback\-blocks = false

clear\-cache\-post\-run = false

confidence = [\(dqHIGH\(dq, \(dqCONTROL_FLOW\(dq, \(dqINFERENCE\(dq, \(dqINFERENCE_FAILURE\(dq, \(dqUNDEFINED\(dq]

disable = [\(dqbad\-inline\-option\(dq, \(dqconsider\-using\-augmented\-assign\(dq, \(dqdeprecated\-pragma\(dq, \(dqfile\-ignored\(dq, \(dqlocally\-disabled\(dq, \(dqprefer\-typing\-namedtuple\(dq, \(dqraw\-checker\-failed\(dq, \(dqsuppressed\-message\(dq, \(dquse\-implicit\-booleaness\-not\-comparison\-to\-string\(dq, \(dquse\-implicit\-booleaness\-not\-comparison\-to\-zero\(dq, \(dquse\-symbolic\-message\-instead\(dq, \(dquseless\-suppression\(dq]

enable = []

evaluation = \(dqmax(0, 0 if fatal else 10.0 \- ((float(5 * error + warning + refactor + convention) / statement) * 10))\(dq

exit\-zero = false

extension\-pkg\-allow\-list = []

extension\-pkg\-whitelist = []

fail\-on = []

fail\-under = 10

from\-stdin = false

ignore = [\(dqCVS\(dq]

ignore\-paths = []

ignore\-patterns = [\(dq^\e\e.#\(dq]

ignored\-modules = []

jobs = 1

limit\-inference\-results = 100

load\-plugins = []

msg\-template = \(dq\(dq

# output\-format =

persistent = true

prefer\-stubs = false

py\-version = \(dqsys.version_info[:2]\(dq

recursive = false

reports = false

score = true

source\-roots = []

unsafe\-load\-any\-extension = false
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBBasic\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-argument\-naming\-style
.sp
\fINaming style matching correct argument names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBsnake_case\fP
.SS \-\-argument\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct argument names. Overrides argument\-naming\-style. If left empty, argument names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-attr\-naming\-style
.sp
\fINaming style matching correct attribute names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBsnake_case\fP
.SS \-\-attr\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct attribute names. Overrides attr\-naming\-style. If left empty, attribute names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-bad\-names
.sp
\fIBad variable names which should always be refused, separated by a comma.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqfoo\(aq, \(aqbar\(aq, \(aqbaz\(aq, \(aqtoto\(aq, \(aqtutu\(aq, \(aqtata\(aq)\fP
.SS \-\-bad\-names\-rgxs
.sp
\fIBad variable names regexes, separated by a comma. If names match any regex, they will always be refused\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.SS \-\-class\-attribute\-naming\-style
.sp
\fINaming style matching correct class attribute names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBany\fP
.SS \-\-class\-attribute\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct class attribute names. Overrides class\-attribute\-naming\-style. If left empty, class attribute names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-class\-const\-naming\-style
.sp
\fINaming style matching correct class constant names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBUPPER_CASE\fP
.SS \-\-class\-const\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct class constant names. Overrides class\-const\-naming\-style. If left empty, class constant names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-class\-naming\-style
.sp
\fINaming style matching correct class names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBPascalCase\fP
.SS \-\-class\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct class names. Overrides class\-naming\-style. If left empty, class names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-const\-naming\-style
.sp
\fINaming style matching correct constant names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBUPPER_CASE\fP
.SS \-\-const\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct constant names. Overrides const\-naming\-style. If left empty, constant names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-docstring\-min\-length
.sp
\fIMinimum line length for functions/classes that require docstrings, shorter ones are exempt.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB\-1\fP
.SS \-\-function\-naming\-style
.sp
\fINaming style matching correct function names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBsnake_case\fP
.SS \-\-function\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct function names. Overrides function\-naming\-style. If left empty, function names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-good\-names
.sp
\fIGood variable names which should always be accepted, separated by a comma.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqi\(aq, \(aqj\(aq, \(aqk\(aq, \(aqex\(aq, \(aqRun\(aq, \(aq_\(aq)\fP
.SS \-\-good\-names\-rgxs
.sp
\fIGood variable names regexes, separated by a comma. If names match any regex, they will always be accepted\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.SS \-\-include\-naming\-hint
.sp
\fIInclude a hint for the correct naming format with invalid\-name.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-inlinevar\-naming\-style
.sp
\fINaming style matching correct inline iteration names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBany\fP
.SS \-\-inlinevar\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct inline iteration names. Overrides inlinevar\-naming\-style. If left empty, inline iteration names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-method\-naming\-style
.sp
\fINaming style matching correct method names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBsnake_case\fP
.SS \-\-method\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct method names. Overrides method\-naming\-style. If left empty, method names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-module\-naming\-style
.sp
\fINaming style matching correct module names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBsnake_case\fP
.SS \-\-module\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct module names. Overrides module\-naming\-style. If left empty, module names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-name\-group
.sp
\fIColon\-delimited sets of names that determine each other\(aqs naming style when the name regexes allow several styles.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-no\-docstring\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression which should only match function or class names that do not require a docstring.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBre.compile(\(aq^_\(aq)\fP
.SS \-\-paramspec\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct parameter specification variable names. If left empty, parameter specification variable names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-property\-classes
.sp
\fIList of decorators that produce properties, such as abc.abstractproperty. Add to this list to register other decorators that produce valid properties. These decorators are taken in consideration only for invalid\-name.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqabc.abstractproperty\(aq,)\fP
.SS \-\-typealias\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct type alias names. If left empty, type alias names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-typevar\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct type variable names. If left empty, type variable names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-typevartuple\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct type variable tuple names. If left empty, type variable tuple names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.SS \-\-variable\-naming\-style
.sp
\fINaming style matching correct variable names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBsnake_case\fP
.SS \-\-variable\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression matching correct variable names. Overrides variable\-naming\-style. If left empty, variable names will be checked with the set naming style.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBNone\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.basic]
# Possible choices: [\(aqsnake_case\(aq, \(aqcamelCase\(aq, \(aqPascalCase\(aq, \(aqUPPER_CASE\(aq, \(aqany\(aq]
argument\-naming\-style = \(dqsnake_case\(dq

# argument\-rgx =

# Possible choices: [\(aqsnake_case\(aq, \(aqcamelCase\(aq, \(aqPascalCase\(aq, \(aqUPPER_CASE\(aq, \(aqany\(aq]
attr\-naming\-style = \(dqsnake_case\(dq

# attr\-rgx =

bad\-names = [\(dqfoo\(dq, \(dqbar\(dq, \(dqbaz\(dq, \(dqtoto\(dq, \(dqtutu\(dq, \(dqtata\(dq]

bad\-names\-rgxs = []

# Possible choices: [\(aqsnake_case\(aq, \(aqcamelCase\(aq, \(aqPascalCase\(aq, \(aqUPPER_CASE\(aq, \(aqany\(aq]
class\-attribute\-naming\-style = \(dqany\(dq

# class\-attribute\-rgx =

# Possible choices: [\(aqsnake_case\(aq, \(aqcamelCase\(aq, \(aqPascalCase\(aq, \(aqUPPER_CASE\(aq, \(aqany\(aq]
class\-const\-naming\-style = \(dqUPPER_CASE\(dq

# class\-const\-rgx =

# Possible choices: [\(aqsnake_case\(aq, \(aqcamelCase\(aq, \(aqPascalCase\(aq, \(aqUPPER_CASE\(aq, \(aqany\(aq]
class\-naming\-style = \(dqPascalCase\(dq

# class\-rgx =

# Possible choices: [\(aqsnake_case\(aq, \(aqcamelCase\(aq, \(aqPascalCase\(aq, \(aqUPPER_CASE\(aq, \(aqany\(aq]
const\-naming\-style = \(dqUPPER_CASE\(dq

# const\-rgx =

docstring\-min\-length = \-1

# Possible choices: [\(aqsnake_case\(aq, \(aqcamelCase\(aq, \(aqPascalCase\(aq, \(aqUPPER_CASE\(aq, \(aqany\(aq]
function\-naming\-style = \(dqsnake_case\(dq

# function\-rgx =

good\-names = [\(dqi\(dq, \(dqj\(dq, \(dqk\(dq, \(dqex\(dq, \(dqRun\(dq, \(dq_\(dq]

good\-names\-rgxs = []

include\-naming\-hint = false

# Possible choices: [\(aqsnake_case\(aq, \(aqcamelCase\(aq, \(aqPascalCase\(aq, \(aqUPPER_CASE\(aq, \(aqany\(aq]
inlinevar\-naming\-style = \(dqany\(dq

# inlinevar\-rgx =

# Possible choices: [\(aqsnake_case\(aq, \(aqcamelCase\(aq, \(aqPascalCase\(aq, \(aqUPPER_CASE\(aq, \(aqany\(aq]
method\-naming\-style = \(dqsnake_case\(dq

# method\-rgx =

# Possible choices: [\(aqsnake_case\(aq, \(aqcamelCase\(aq, \(aqPascalCase\(aq, \(aqUPPER_CASE\(aq, \(aqany\(aq]
module\-naming\-style = \(dqsnake_case\(dq

# module\-rgx =

name\-group = []

no\-docstring\-rgx = \(dq^_\(dq

# paramspec\-rgx =

property\-classes = [\(dqabc.abstractproperty\(dq]

# typealias\-rgx =

# typevar\-rgx =

# typevartuple\-rgx =

# Possible choices: [\(aqsnake_case\(aq, \(aqcamelCase\(aq, \(aqPascalCase\(aq, \(aqUPPER_CASE\(aq, \(aqany\(aq]
variable\-naming\-style = \(dqsnake_case\(dq

# variable\-rgx =
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBClasses\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-check\-protected\-access\-in\-special\-methods
.sp
\fIWarn about protected attribute access inside special methods\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-defining\-attr\-methods
.sp
\fIList of method names used to declare (i.e. assign) instance attributes.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aq__init__\(aq, \(aq__new__\(aq, \(aqsetUp\(aq, \(aqasyncSetUp\(aq, \(aq__post_init__\(aq)\fP
.SS \-\-exclude\-protected
.sp
\fIList of member names, which should be excluded from the protected access warning.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aq_asdict\(aq, \(aq_fields\(aq, \(aq_replace\(aq, \(aq_source\(aq, \(aq_make\(aq, \(aqos._exit\(aq)\fP
.SS \-\-valid\-classmethod\-first\-arg
.sp
\fIList of valid names for the first argument in a class method.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqcls\(aq,)\fP
.SS \-\-valid\-metaclass\-classmethod\-first\-arg
.sp
\fIList of valid names for the first argument in a metaclass class method.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqmcs\(aq,)\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.classes]
check\-protected\-access\-in\-special\-methods = false

defining\-attr\-methods = [\(dq__init__\(dq, \(dq__new__\(dq, \(dqsetUp\(dq, \(dqasyncSetUp\(dq, \(dq__post_init__\(dq]

exclude\-protected = [\(dq_asdict\(dq, \(dq_fields\(dq, \(dq_replace\(dq, \(dq_source\(dq, \(dq_make\(dq, \(dqos._exit\(dq]

valid\-classmethod\-first\-arg = [\(dqcls\(dq]

valid\-metaclass\-classmethod\-first\-arg = [\(dqmcs\(dq]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBDesign\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-exclude\-too\-few\-public\-methods
.sp
\fIList of regular expressions of class ancestor names to ignore when counting public methods (see R0903)\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB[]\fP
.SS \-\-ignored\-parents
.sp
\fIList of qualified class names to ignore when counting class parents (see R0901)\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-max\-args
.sp
\fIMaximum number of arguments for function / method.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB5\fP
.SS \-\-max\-attributes
.sp
\fIMaximum number of attributes for a class (see R0902).\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB7\fP
.SS \-\-max\-bool\-expr
.sp
\fIMaximum number of boolean expressions in an if statement (see R0916).\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB5\fP
.SS \-\-max\-branches
.sp
\fIMaximum number of branch for function / method body.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB12\fP
.SS \-\-max\-complexity
.sp
\fIMcCabe complexity cyclomatic threshold\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB10\fP
.SS \-\-max\-locals
.sp
\fIMaximum number of locals for function / method body.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB15\fP
.SS \-\-max\-parents
.sp
\fIMaximum number of parents for a class (see R0901).\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB7\fP
.SS \-\-max\-positional\-arguments
.sp
\fIMaximum number of positional arguments for function / method.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB5\fP
.SS \-\-max\-public\-methods
.sp
\fIMaximum number of public methods for a class (see R0904).\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB20\fP
.SS \-\-max\-returns
.sp
\fIMaximum number of return / yield for function / method body.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB6\fP
.SS \-\-max\-statements
.sp
\fIMaximum number of statements in function / method body.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB50\fP
.SS \-\-min\-public\-methods
.sp
\fIMinimum number of public methods for a class (see R0903).\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB2\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.design]
exclude\-too\-few\-public\-methods = []

ignored\-parents = []

max\-args = 5

max\-attributes = 7

max\-bool\-expr = 5

max\-branches = 12

max\-complexity = 10

max\-locals = 15

max\-parents = 7

max\-positional\-arguments = 5

max\-public\-methods = 20

max\-returns = 6

max\-statements = 50

min\-public\-methods = 2
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBExceptions\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-overgeneral\-exceptions
.sp
\fIExceptions that will emit a warning when caught.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqbuiltins.BaseException\(aq, \(aqbuiltins.Exception\(aq)\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.exceptions]
overgeneral\-exceptions = [\(dqbuiltins.BaseException\(dq, \(dqbuiltins.Exception\(dq]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBFormat\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-expected\-line\-ending\-format
.sp
\fIExpected format of line ending, e.g. empty (any line ending), LF or CRLF.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.SS \-\-ignore\-long\-lines
.sp
\fIRegexp for a line that is allowed to be longer than the limit.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB^\es*(# )?<?https?://\eS+>?$\fP
.SS \-\-indent\-after\-paren
.sp
\fINumber of spaces of indent required inside a hanging or continued line.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB4\fP
.SS \-\-indent\-string
.sp
\fIString used as indentation unit. This is usually \(dq    \(dq (4 spaces) or \(dqt\(dq (1 tab).\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \(ga\(ga    \(ga\(ga
.SS \-\-max\-line\-length
.sp
\fIMaximum number of characters on a single line. Pylint\(aqs default of 100 is based on PEP 8\(aqs guidance that teams may choose line lengths up to 99 characters.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB100\fP
.SS \-\-max\-module\-lines
.sp
\fIMaximum number of lines in a module.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB1000\fP
.SS \-\-single\-line\-class\-stmt
.sp
\fIAllow the body of a class to be on the same line as the declaration if body contains single statement.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-single\-line\-if\-stmt
.sp
\fIAllow the body of an if to be on the same line as the test if there is no else.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.format]
# Possible choices: [\(aq\(aq, \(aqLF\(aq, \(aqCRLF\(aq]
expected\-line\-ending\-format = \(dq\(dq

ignore\-long\-lines = \(dq^\e\es*(# )?<?https?://\e\eS+>?$\(dq

indent\-after\-paren = 4

indent\-string = \(dq    \(dq

max\-line\-length = 100

max\-module\-lines = 1000

single\-line\-class\-stmt = false

single\-line\-if\-stmt = false
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBImports\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-allow\-any\-import\-level
.sp
\fIList of modules that can be imported at any level, not just the top level one.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-allow\-reexport\-from\-package
.sp
\fIAllow explicit reexports by alias from a package __init__.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-allow\-wildcard\-with\-all
.sp
\fIAllow wildcard imports from modules that define __all__.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-deprecated\-modules
.sp
\fIDeprecated modules which should not be used, separated by a comma.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-ext\-import\-graph
.sp
\fIOutput a graph (.gv or any supported image format) of external dependencies to the given file (report RP0402 must not be disabled).\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.SS \-\-import\-graph
.sp
\fIOutput a graph (.gv or any supported image format) of all (i.e. internal and external) dependencies to the given file (report RP0402 must not be disabled).\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.SS \-\-int\-import\-graph
.sp
\fIOutput a graph (.gv or any supported image format) of internal dependencies to the given file (report RP0402 must not be disabled).\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.SS \-\-known\-standard\-library
.sp
\fIForce import order to recognize a module as part of the standard compatibility libraries.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-known\-third\-party
.sp
\fIForce import order to recognize a module as part of a third party library.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqenchant\(aq,)\fP
.SS \-\-preferred\-modules
.sp
\fICouples of modules and preferred modules, separated by a comma.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.imports]
allow\-any\-import\-level = []

allow\-reexport\-from\-package = false

allow\-wildcard\-with\-all = false

deprecated\-modules = []

ext\-import\-graph = \(dq\(dq

import\-graph = \(dq\(dq

int\-import\-graph = \(dq\(dq

known\-standard\-library = []

known\-third\-party = [\(dqenchant\(dq]

preferred\-modules = []
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBLogging\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-logging\-format\-style
.sp
\fIThe type of string formatting that logging methods do. \(gaold\(ga means using % formatting, \(ganew\(ga is for \(ga{}\(ga formatting.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBold\fP
.SS \-\-logging\-modules
.sp
\fILogging modules to check that the string format arguments are in logging function parameter format.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqlogging\(aq,)\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.logging]
# Possible choices: [\(aqold\(aq, \(aqnew\(aq]
logging\-format\-style = \(dqold\(dq

logging\-modules = [\(dqlogging\(dq]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBMethod_args\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-timeout\-methods
.sp
\fIList of qualified names (i.e., library.method) which require a timeout parameter e.g. \(aqrequests.api.get,requests.api.post\(aq\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqrequests.api.delete\(aq, \(aqrequests.api.get\(aq, \(aqrequests.api.head\(aq, \(aqrequests.api.options\(aq, \(aqrequests.api.patch\(aq, \(aqrequests.api.post\(aq, \(aqrequests.api.put\(aq, \(aqrequests.api.request\(aq)\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.method_args]
timeout\-methods = [\(dqrequests.api.delete\(dq, \(dqrequests.api.get\(dq, \(dqrequests.api.head\(dq, \(dqrequests.api.options\(dq, \(dqrequests.api.patch\(dq, \(dqrequests.api.post\(dq, \(dqrequests.api.put\(dq, \(dqrequests.api.request\(dq]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBMiscellaneous\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-check\-fixme\-in\-docstring
.sp
\fIWhether or not to search for fixme\(aqs in docstrings.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-notes
.sp
\fIList of note tags to take in consideration, separated by a comma.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqFIXME\(aq, \(aqXXX\(aq, \(aqTODO\(aq)\fP
.SS \-\-notes\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegular expression of note tags to take in consideration.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.miscellaneous]
check\-fixme\-in\-docstring = false

notes = [\(dqFIXME\(dq, \(dqXXX\(dq, \(dqTODO\(dq]

notes\-rgx = \(dq\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBRefactoring\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-max\-nested\-blocks
.sp
\fIMaximum number of nested blocks for function / method body\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB5\fP
.SS \-\-never\-returning\-functions
.sp
\fIComplete name of functions that never returns. When checking for inconsistent\-return\-statements if a never returning function is called then it will be considered as an explicit return statement and no message will be printed.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqsys.exit\(aq, \(aqargparse.parse_error\(aq)\fP
.SS \-\-suggest\-join\-with\-non\-empty\-separator
.sp
\fILet \(aqconsider\-using\-join\(aq be raised when the separator to join on would be non\-empty (resulting in expected fixes of the type: \(ga\(ga\(dq\- \(dq + \(dq
\- \(dq.join(items)\(ga\(ga)\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.refactoring]
max\-nested\-blocks = 5

never\-returning\-functions = [\(dqsys.exit\(dq, \(dqargparse.parse_error\(dq]

suggest\-join\-with\-non\-empty\-separator = true
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBSimilarities\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-ignore\-comments
.sp
\fIComments are removed from the similarity computation\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-ignore\-docstrings
.sp
\fIDocstrings are removed from the similarity computation\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-ignore\-imports
.sp
\fIImports are removed from the similarity computation\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-ignore\-signatures
.sp
\fISignatures are removed from the similarity computation\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-min\-similarity\-lines
.sp
\fIMinimum lines number of a similarity.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB4\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.similarities]
ignore\-comments = true

ignore\-docstrings = true

ignore\-imports = true

ignore\-signatures = true

min\-similarity\-lines = 4
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBSpelling\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-max\-spelling\-suggestions
.sp
\fILimits count of emitted suggestions for spelling mistakes.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB4\fP
.SS \-\-spelling\-dict
.sp
\fISpelling dictionary name. Available dictionaries depends on your local enchant installation\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.SS \-\-spelling\-ignore\-comment\-directives
.sp
\fIList of comma separated words that should be considered directives if they appear at the beginning of a comment and should not be checked.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBfmt: on,fmt: off,noqa:,noqa,nosec,isort:skip,mypy:\fP
.SS \-\-spelling\-ignore\-words
.sp
\fIList of comma separated words that should not be checked.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.SS \-\-spelling\-private\-dict\-file
.sp
\fIA path to a file that contains the private dictionary; one word per line.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP \fB\(dq\(dq\fP
.SS \-\-spelling\-store\-unknown\-words
.sp
\fITells whether to store unknown words to the private dictionary (see the \-\-spelling\-private\-dict\-file option) instead of raising a message.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBn\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.spelling]
max\-spelling\-suggestions = 4

# Possible choices: Values from \(aqenchant.Broker().list_dicts()\(aq depending on your local enchant installation
spelling\-dict = \(dq\(dq

spelling\-ignore\-comment\-directives = \(dqfmt: on,fmt: off,noqa:,noqa,nosec,isort:skip,mypy:\(dq

spelling\-ignore\-words = \(dq\(dq

spelling\-private\-dict\-file = \(dq\(dq

spelling\-store\-unknown\-words = false
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBString\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-check\-quote\-consistency
.sp
\fIThis flag controls whether inconsistent\-quotes generates a warning when the character used as a quote delimiter is used inconsistently within a module.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-check\-str\-concat\-over\-line\-jumps
.sp
\fIThis flag controls whether the implicit\-str\-concat should generate a warning on implicit string concatenation in sequences defined over several lines.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.string]
check\-quote\-consistency = false

check\-str\-concat\-over\-line\-jumps = false
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBTypecheck\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-contextmanager\-decorators
.sp
\fIList of decorators that produce context managers, such as contextlib.contextmanager. Add to this list to register other decorators that produce valid context managers.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB[\(aqcontextlib.contextmanager\(aq]\fP
.SS \-\-generated\-members
.sp
\fIList of members which are set dynamically and missed by pylint inference system, and so shouldn\(aqt trigger E1101 when accessed. Python regular expressions are accepted.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-ignore\-mixin\-members
.sp
\fITells whether missing members accessed in mixin class should be ignored. A class is considered mixin if its name matches the mixin\-class\-rgx option.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-ignore\-none
.sp
\fITells whether to warn about missing members when the owner of the attribute is inferred to be None.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-ignore\-on\-opaque\-inference
.sp
\fIThis flag controls whether pylint should warn about no\-member and similar checks whenever an opaque object is returned when inferring. The inference can return multiple potential results while evaluating a Python object, but some branches might not be evaluated, which results in partial inference. In that case, it might be useful to still emit no\-member and other checks for the rest of the inferred objects.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-ignored\-checks\-for\-mixins
.sp
\fIList of symbolic message names to ignore for Mixin members.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB[\(aqno\-member\(aq, \(aqnot\-async\-context\-manager\(aq, \(aqnot\-context\-manager\(aq, \(aqattribute\-defined\-outside\-init\(aq]\fP
.SS \-\-ignored\-classes
.sp
\fIList of class names for which member attributes should not be checked (useful for classes with dynamically set attributes). This supports the use of qualified names.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqoptparse.Values\(aq, \(aqthread._local\(aq, \(aq_thread._local\(aq, \(aqargparse.Namespace\(aq)\fP
.SS \-\-missing\-member\-hint
.sp
\fIShow a hint with possible names when a member name was not found. The aspect of finding the hint is based on edit distance.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-missing\-member\-hint\-distance
.sp
\fIThe maximum edit distance a name should have in order to be considered a similar match for a missing member name.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB1\fP
.SS \-\-missing\-member\-max\-choices
.sp
\fIThe total number of similar names that should be taken in consideration when showing a hint for a missing member.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB1\fP
.SS \-\-mixin\-class\-rgx
.sp
\fIRegex pattern to define which classes are considered mixins.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB\&.*[Mm]ixin\fP
.SS \-\-signature\-mutators
.sp
\fIList of decorators that change the signature of a decorated function.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB[]\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.typecheck]
contextmanager\-decorators = [\(dqcontextlib.contextmanager\(dq]

generated\-members = []

ignore\-mixin\-members = true

ignore\-none = true

ignore\-on\-opaque\-inference = true

ignored\-checks\-for\-mixins = [\(dqno\-member\(dq, \(dqnot\-async\-context\-manager\(dq, \(dqnot\-context\-manager\(dq, \(dqattribute\-defined\-outside\-init\(dq]

ignored\-classes = [\(dqoptparse.Values\(dq, \(dqthread._local\(dq, \(dq_thread._local\(dq, \(dqargparse.Namespace\(dq]

missing\-member\-hint = true

missing\-member\-hint\-distance = 1

missing\-member\-max\-choices = 1

mixin\-class\-rgx = \(dq.*[Mm]ixin\(dq

signature\-mutators = []
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBVariables\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-additional\-builtins
.sp
\fIList of additional names supposed to be defined in builtins. Remember that you should avoid defining new builtins when possible.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-allow\-global\-unused\-variables
.sp
\fITells whether unused global variables should be treated as a violation.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-allowed\-redefined\-builtins
.sp
\fIList of names allowed to shadow builtins\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB()\fP
.SS \-\-callbacks
.sp
\fIList of strings which can identify a callback function by name. A callback name must start or end with one of those strings.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqcb_\(aq, \(aq_cb\(aq)\fP
.SS \-\-dummy\-variables\-rgx
.sp
\fIA regular expression matching the name of dummy variables (i.e. expected to not be used).\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB_+$|(_[a\-zA\-Z0\-9_]*[a\-zA\-Z0\-9]+?$)|dummy|^ignored_|^unused_\fP
.SS \-\-ignored\-argument\-names
.sp
\fIArgument names that match this expression will be ignored.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBre.compile(\(aq_.*|^ignored_|^unused_\(aq)\fP
.SS \-\-init\-import
.sp
\fITells whether we should check for unused import in __init__ files.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBFalse\fP
.SS \-\-redefining\-builtins\-modules
.sp
\fIList of qualified module names which can have objects that can redefine builtins.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(\(aqsix.moves\(aq, \(aqpast.builtins\(aq, \(aqfuture.builtins\(aq, \(aqbuiltins\(aq, \(aqio\(aq)\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.variables]
additional\-builtins = []

allow\-global\-unused\-variables = true

allowed\-redefined\-builtins = []

callbacks = [\(dqcb_\(dq, \(dq_cb\(dq]

dummy\-variables\-rgx = \(dq_+$|(_[a\-zA\-Z0\-9_]*[a\-zA\-Z0\-9]+?$)|dummy|^ignored_|^unused_\(dq

ignored\-argument\-names = \(dq_.*|^ignored_|^unused_\(dq

init\-import = false

redefining\-builtins\-modules = [\(dqsix.moves\(dq, \(dqpast.builtins\(dq, \(dqfuture.builtins\(dq, \(dqbuiltins\(dq, \(dqio\(dq]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Extensions
.SS \fBBroad_try_clause\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-max\-try\-statements
.sp
\fIMaximum number of statements allowed in a try clause\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB1\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.broad_try_clause]
max\-try\-statements = 1
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBCode_style\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-max\-line\-length\-suggestions
.sp
\fIMax line length for which to sill emit suggestions. Used to prevent optional suggestions which would get split by a code formatter (e.g., black). Will default to the setting for \(ga\(gamax\-line\-length\(ga\(ga.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB0\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.code_style]
max\-line\-length\-suggestions = 0
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBDeprecated_builtins\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-bad\-functions
.sp
\fIList of builtins function names that should not be used, separated by a comma\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB[\(aqmap\(aq, \(aqfilter\(aq]\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.deprecated_builtins]
bad\-functions = [\(dqmap\(dq, \(dqfilter\(dq]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBDunder\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-good\-dunder\-names
.sp
\fIGood dunder names which should always be accepted.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB[]\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.dunder]
good\-dunder\-names = []
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBMagic\-value\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-valid\-magic\-values
.sp
\fIList of valid magic values that \(gamagic\-value\-compare\(ga will not detect. Supports integers, floats, negative numbers, for empty string enter \(ga\(ga\(aq\(aq\(ga\(ga, for backslash values just use one backslash e.g n.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fB(0, \-1, 1, \(aq\(aq, \(aq__main__\(aq)\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.magic\-value]
valid\-magic\-values = [0, \-1, 1, \(dq\(dq, \(dq__main__\(dq]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBParameter_documentation\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-accept\-no\-param\-doc
.sp
\fIWhether to accept totally missing parameter documentation in the docstring of a function that has parameters.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-accept\-no\-raise\-doc
.sp
\fIWhether to accept totally missing raises documentation in the docstring of a function that raises an exception.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-accept\-no\-return\-doc
.sp
\fIWhether to accept totally missing return documentation in the docstring of a function that returns a statement.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-accept\-no\-yields\-doc
.sp
\fIWhether to accept totally missing yields documentation in the docstring of a generator.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.SS \-\-default\-docstring\-type
.sp
\fIIf the docstring type cannot be guessed the specified docstring type will be used.\fP
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBdefault\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.parameter_documentation]
accept\-no\-param\-doc = true

accept\-no\-raise\-doc = true

accept\-no\-return\-doc = true

accept\-no\-yields\-doc = true

# Possible choices: [\(aqsphinx\(aq, \(aqepytext\(aq, \(aqgoogle\(aq, \(aqnumpy\(aq, \(aqdefault\(aq]
default\-docstring\-type = \(dqdefault\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBTyping\fP \fBChecker\fP
.SS \-\-runtime\-typing
.sp
\fISet to \(ga\(gano\(ga\(ga if the app / library does **NOT*\fP need to support runtime introspection of type annotations. If you use type annotations \fBexclusively\fP for type checking of an application, you\(aqre probably fine. For libraries, evaluate if some users want to access the type hints at runtime first, e.g., through \fBtyping.get_type_hints\fP\&. Applies to Python versions 3.7 \- 3.9*
.sp
\fBDefault:\fP  \fBTrue\fP
.sp
\fBNote:\fP Only \fBtool.pylint\fP is required, the section title is not. These are the default values.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[tool.pylint.typing]
runtime\-typing = true
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Checkers
.SS Pylint features
.SS Pylint checkers\(aq options and switches
.sp
Pylint checkers can provide three set of features:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
options that control their execution,
.IP \(bu 2
messages that they can raise,
.IP \(bu 2
reports that they can generate.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Below is a list of all checkers and their features.
.SS Async checker
.sp
Verbatim name of the checker is \fBasync\fP\&.
.SS Async checker Messages
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B yield\-inside\-async\-function (E1700)
\fIYield inside async function\fP
Used when an \fIyield\fP or \fIyield from\fP statement is found inside an async
function. This message can\(aqt be emitted when using Python < 3.5.
.TP
.B not\-async\-context\-manager (E1701)
\fIAsync context manager \(aq%s\(aq doesn\(aqt implement __aenter__ and __aexit__.\fP
Used when an async context manager is used with an object that does not
implement the async context management protocol. This message can\(aqt be
emitted when using Python < 3.5.
.UNINDENT
.SS Bad\-Chained\-Comparison checker
.sp
Verbatim name of the checker is \fBbad\-chained\-comparison\fP\&.
.SS Bad\-Chained\-Comparison checker Messages
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B bad\-chained\-comparison (W3601)
\fISuspicious %s\-part chained comparison using semantically incompatible operators (%s)\fP
Used when there is a chained comparison where one expression is part of two
comparisons that belong to different semantic groups (\(dq<\(dq does not mean the
same thing as \(dqis\(dq, chaining them in \(dq0 < x is None\(dq is probably a mistake).
.UNINDENT
.SS Basic checker
.sp
Verbatim name of the checker is \fBbasic\fP\&.
.sp
See also basic checker\(aqs options\(aq documentation \%<#\:basic-options>
.SS Basic checker Messages
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B init\-is\-generator (E0100)
\fI__init__ method is a generator\fP
Used when the special class method __init__ is turned into a generator by a
yield in its body.
.TP
.B return\-in\-init (E0101)
\fIExplicit return in __init__\fP
Used when the special class method __init__ has an explicit return value.
.TP
.B function\-redefined (E0102)
\fI%s already defined line %s\fP
Used when a function / class / method is redefined.
.TP
.B not\-in\-loop (E0103)
\fI%r not properly in loop\fP
Used when break or continue keywords are used outside a loop.
.TP
.B return\-outside\-function (E0104)
\fIReturn outside function\fP
Used when a \(dqreturn\(dq statement is found outside a function or method.
.TP
.B yield\-outside\-function (E0105)
\fIYield outside function\fP
Used when a \(dqyield\(dq statement is found outside a function or method.
.TP
.B return\-arg\-in\-generator (E0106)
\fIReturn with argument inside generator\fP
Used when a \(dqreturn\(dq statement with an argument is found in a generator
function or method (e.g. with some \(dqyield\(dq statements). This message can\(aqt be
emitted when using Python >= 3.3.
.TP
.B nonexistent\-operator (E0107)
\fIUse of the non\-existent %s operator\fP
Used when you attempt to use the C\-style pre\-increment or pre\-decrement
operator \-\- and ++, which doesn\(aqt exist in Python.
.TP
.B duplicate\-argument\-name (E0108)
\fIDuplicate argument name %r in function definition\fP
Duplicate argument names in function definitions are syntax errors.
.TP
.B abstract\-class\-instantiated (E0110)
\fIAbstract class %r with abstract methods instantiated\fP
Used when an abstract class with \fIabc.ABCMeta\fP as metaclass has abstract
methods and is instantiated.
.TP
.B bad\-reversed\-sequence (E0111)
\fIThe first reversed() argument is not a sequence\fP
Used when the first argument to reversed() builtin isn\(aqt a sequence (does not
implement __reversed__, nor __getitem__ and __len__
.TP
.B too\-many\-star\-expressions (E0112)
\fIMore than one starred expression in assignment\fP
Emitted when there are more than one starred expressions (\fI*x\fP) in an
assignment. This is a SyntaxError.
.TP
.B invalid\-star\-assignment\-target (E0113)
\fIStarred assignment target must be in a list or tuple\fP
Emitted when a star expression is used as a starred assignment target.
.TP
.B star\-needs\-assignment\-target (E0114)
\fICan use starred expression only in assignment target\fP
Emitted when a star expression is not used in an assignment target.
.TP
.B nonlocal\-and\-global (E0115)
\fIName %r is nonlocal and global\fP
Emitted when a name is both nonlocal and global.
.TP
.B nonlocal\-without\-binding (E0117)
\fInonlocal name %s found without binding\fP
Emitted when a nonlocal variable does not have an attached name somewhere in
the parent scopes
.TP
.B used\-prior\-global\-declaration (E0118)
\fIName %r is used prior to global declaration\fP
Emitted when a name is used prior a global declaration, which results in an
error since Python 3.6. This message can\(aqt be emitted when using Python <
3.6.
.TP
.B misplaced\-format\-function (E0119)
\fIformat function is not called on str\fP
Emitted when format function is not called on str object. e.g doing
print(\(dqvalue: {}\(dq).format(123) instead of print(\(dqvalue: {}\(dq.format(123)).
This might not be what the user intended to do.
.TP
.B unreachable (W0101)
\fIUnreachable code\fP
Used when there is some code behind a \(dqreturn\(dq or \(dqraise\(dq statement, which
will never be accessed.
.TP
.B dangerous\-default\-value (W0102)
\fIDangerous default value %s as argument\fP
Used when a mutable value as list or dictionary is detected in a default
value for an argument.
.TP
.B pointless\-statement (W0104)
\fIStatement seems to have no effect\fP
Used when a statement doesn\(aqt have (or at least seems to) any effect.
.TP
.B pointless\-string\-statement (W0105)
\fIString statement has no effect\fP
Used when a string is used as a statement (which of course has no effect).
This is a particular case of W0104 with its own message so you can easily
disable it if you\(aqre using those strings as documentation, instead of
comments.
.TP
.B expression\-not\-assigned (W0106)
\fIExpression \(dq%s\(dq is assigned to nothing\fP
Used when an expression that is not a function call is assigned to nothing.
Probably something else was intended.
.TP
.B unnecessary\-pass (W0107)
\fIUnnecessary pass statement\fP
Used when a \(dqpass\(dq statement can be removed without affecting the behaviour
of the code.
.TP
.B unnecessary\-lambda (W0108)
\fILambda may not be necessary\fP
Used when the body of a lambda expression is a function call on the same
argument list as the lambda itself; such lambda expressions are in all but a
few cases replaceable with the function being called in the body of the
lambda.
.TP
.B duplicate\-key (W0109)
\fIDuplicate key %r in dictionary\fP
Used when a dictionary expression binds the same key multiple times.
.TP
.B useless\-else\-on\-loop (W0120)
\fIElse clause on loop without a break statement, remove the else and de\-indent all the code inside it\fP
Loops should only have an else clause if they can exit early with a break
statement, otherwise the statements under else should be on the same scope as
the loop itself.
.TP
.B exec\-used (W0122)
\fIUse of exec\fP
Raised when the \(aqexec\(aq statement is used. It\(aqs dangerous to use this function
for a user input, and it\(aqs also slower than actual code in general. This
doesn\(aqt mean you should never use it, but you should consider alternatives
first and restrict the functions available.
.TP
.B eval\-used (W0123)
\fIUse of eval\fP
Used when you use the \(dqeval\(dq function, to discourage its usage. Consider
using \fIast.literal_eval\fP for safely evaluating strings containing Python
expressions from untrusted sources.
.TP
.B confusing\-with\-statement (W0124)
\fIFollowing \(dqas\(dq with another context manager looks like a tuple.\fP
Emitted when a \fIwith\fP statement component returns multiple values and uses
name binding with \fIas\fP only for a part of those values, as in with ctx() as
a, b. This can be misleading, since it\(aqs not clear if the context manager
returns a tuple or if the node without a name binding is another context
manager.
.TP
.B using\-constant\-test (W0125)
\fIUsing a conditional statement with a constant value\fP
Emitted when a conditional statement (If or ternary if) uses a constant value
for its test. This might not be what the user intended to do.
.TP
.B missing\-parentheses\-for\-call\-in\-test (W0126)
\fIUsing a conditional statement with potentially wrong function or method call due to missing parentheses\fP
Emitted when a conditional statement (If or ternary if) seems to wrongly call
a function due to missing parentheses
.TP
.B self\-assigning\-variable (W0127)
\fIAssigning the same variable %r to itself\fP
Emitted when we detect that a variable is assigned to itself
.TP
.B redeclared\-assigned\-name (W0128)
\fIRedeclared variable %r in assignment\fP
Emitted when we detect that a variable was redeclared in the same assignment.
.TP
.B assert\-on\-string\-literal (W0129)
\fIAssert statement has a string literal as its first argument. The assert will %s fail.\fP
Used when an assert statement has a string literal as its first argument,
which will cause the assert to always pass.
.TP
.B duplicate\-value (W0130)
\fIDuplicate value %r in set\fP
This message is emitted when a set contains the same value two or more times.
.TP
.B named\-expr\-without\-context (W0131)
\fINamed expression used without context\fP
Emitted if named expression is used to do a regular assignment outside a
context like if, for, while, or a comprehension.
.TP
.B pointless\-exception\-statement (W0133)
\fIException statement has no effect\fP
Used when an exception is created without being assigned, raised or returned
for subsequent use elsewhere.
.TP
.B return\-in\-finally (W0134)
\fI\(aqreturn\(aq shadowed by the \(aqfinally\(aq clause.\fP
Emitted when a \(aqreturn\(aq statement is found in a \(aqfinally\(aq block. This will
overwrite the return value of a function and should be avoided.
.TP
.B contextmanager\-generator\-missing\-cleanup (W0135)
\fIThe context used in function %r will not be exited.\fP
Used when a contextmanager is used inside a generator function and the
cleanup is not handled.
.TP
.B continue\-in\-finally (W0136)
\fI\(aqcontinue\(aq discouraged inside \(aqfinally\(aq clause\fP
Emitted when the \fIcontinue\fP keyword is found inside a finally clause. This
will raise a SyntaxWarning starting in Python 3.14.
.TP
.B break\-in\-finally (W0137)
\fI\(aqbreak\(aq discouraged inside \(aqfinally\(aq clause\fP
Emitted when the \fIbreak\fP keyword is found inside a finally clause. This will
raise a SyntaxWarning starting in Python 3.14.
.TP
.B comparison\-with\-callable (W0143)
\fIComparing against a callable, did you omit the parenthesis?\fP
This message is emitted when pylint detects that a comparison with a callable
was made, which might suggest that some parenthesis were omitted, resulting
in potential unwanted behaviour.
.TP
.B lost\-exception (W0150)
\fI%s statement in finally block may swallow exception\fP
Used when a break or a return statement is found inside the finally clause of
a try...finally block: the exceptions raised in the try clause will be
silently swallowed instead of being re\-raised.
.TP
.B nan\-comparison (W0177)
\fIComparison %s should be %s\fP
Used when an expression is compared to NaN values like numpy.NaN and
float(\(aqnan\(aq).
.TP
.B assert\-on\-tuple (W0199)
\fIAssert called on a populated tuple. Did you mean \(aqassert x,y\(aq?\fP
A call of assert on a tuple will always evaluate to true if the tuple is not
empty, and will always evaluate to false if it is.
.TP
.B literal\-comparison (R0123)
\fIIn \(aq%s\(aq, use \(aq%s\(aq when comparing constant literals not \(aq%s\(aq (\(aq%s\(aq)\fP
Used when comparing an object to a literal, which is usually what you do not
want to do, since you can compare to a different literal than what was
expected altogether.
.TP
.B comparison\-with\-itself (R0124)
\fIRedundant comparison \- %s\fP
Used when something is compared against itself.
.TP
.B comparison\-of\-constants (R0133)
\fIComparison between constants: \(dq%s %s %s\(dq has a constant value\fP
When two literals are compared with each other the result is a constant.
Using the constant directly is both easier to read and more performant.
Initializing \(aqTrue\(aq and \(aqFalse\(aq this way is not required since Python 2.3.
.TP
.B invalid\-name (C0103)
\fI%s name \(dq%s\(dq doesn\(aqt conform to %s\fP
Used when the name doesn\(aqt conform to naming rules associated to its type
(constant, variable, class...).
.TP
.B disallowed\-name (C0104)
\fIDisallowed name \(dq%s\(dq\fP
Used when the name matches bad\-names or bad\-names\-rgxs\- (unauthorized names).
.TP
.B typevar\-name\-incorrect\-variance (C0105)
\fIType variable name does not reflect variance%s\fP
Emitted when a TypeVar name doesn\(aqt reflect its type variance. According to
PEP8, it is recommended to add suffixes \(aq_co\(aq and \(aq_contra\(aq to the variables
used to declare covariant or contravariant behaviour respectively. Invariant
(default) variables do not require a suffix. The message is also emitted when
invariant variables do have a suffix.
.TP
.B empty\-docstring (C0112)
\fIEmpty %s docstring\fP
Used when a module, function, class or method has an empty docstring (it
would be too easy ;).
.TP
.B missing\-module\-docstring (C0114)
\fIMissing module docstring\fP
Used when a module has no docstring. Empty modules do not require a
docstring.
.TP
.B missing\-class\-docstring (C0115)
\fIMissing class docstring\fP
Used when a class has no docstring. Even an empty class must have a
docstring.
.TP
.B missing\-function\-docstring (C0116)
\fIMissing function or method docstring\fP
Used when a function or method has no docstring. Some special methods like
__init__ do not require a docstring.
.TP
.B singleton\-comparison (C0121)
\fIComparison %s should be %s\fP
Used when an expression is compared to singleton values like True, False or
None.
.TP
.B unidiomatic\-typecheck (C0123)
\fIUse isinstance() rather than type() for a typecheck.\fP
The idiomatic way to perform an explicit typecheck in Python is to use
isinstance(x, Y) rather than type(x) == Y, type(x) is Y. Though there are
unusual situations where these give different results.
.TP
.B typevar\-double\-variance (C0131)
\fITypeVar cannot be both covariant and contravariant\fP
Emitted when both the \(dqcovariant\(dq and \(dqcontravariant\(dq keyword arguments are
set to \(dqTrue\(dq in a TypeVar.
.TP
.B typevar\-name\-mismatch (C0132)
\fITypeVar name \(dq%s\(dq does not match assigned variable name \(dq%s\(dq\fP
Emitted when a TypeVar is assigned to a variable that does not match its name
argument.
.UNINDENT
.SS Basic checker Reports
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B RP0101
Statistics by type
.UNINDENT
.SS Classes checker
.sp
Verbatim name of the checker is \fBclasses\fP\&.
.sp
See also classes checker\(aqs options\(aq documentation \%<#\:classes-options>
.SS Classes checker Messages
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B method\-hidden (E0202)
\fIAn attribute defined in %s line %s hides this method\fP
Used when a class defines a method which is hidden by an instance attribute
from an ancestor class or set by some client code.
.TP
.B access\-member\-before\-definition (E0203)
\fIAccess to member %r before its definition line %s\fP
Used when an instance member is accessed before it\(aqs actually assigned.
.TP
.B no\-method\-argument (E0211)
\fIMethod %r has no argument\fP
Used when a method which should have the bound instance as first argument has
no argument defined.
.TP
.B no\-self\-argument (E0213)
\fIMethod %r should have \(dqself\(dq as first argument\fP
Used when a method has an attribute different the \(dqself\(dq as first argument.
This is considered as an error since this is a so common convention that you
shouldn\(aqt break it!
.TP
.B invalid\-slots\-object (E0236)
\fIInvalid object %r in __slots__, must contain only non empty strings\fP
Used when an invalid (non\-string) object occurs in __slots__.
.TP
.B assigning\-non\-slot (E0237)
\fIAssigning to attribute %r not defined in class slots\fP
Used when assigning to an attribute not defined in the class slots.
.TP
.B invalid\-slots (E0238)
\fIInvalid __slots__ object\fP
Used when an invalid __slots__ is found in class. Only a string, an iterable
or a sequence is permitted.
.TP
.B inherit\-non\-class (E0239)
\fIInheriting %r, which is not a class.\fP
Used when a class inherits from something which is not a class.
.TP
.B inconsistent\-mro (E0240)
\fIInconsistent method resolution order for class %r\fP
Used when a class has an inconsistent method resolution order.
.TP
.B duplicate\-bases (E0241)
\fIDuplicate bases for class %r\fP
Duplicate use of base classes in derived classes raise TypeErrors.
.TP
.B class\-variable\-slots\-conflict (E0242)
\fIValue %r in slots conflicts with class variable\fP
Used when a value in __slots__ conflicts with a class variable, property or
method.
.TP
.B invalid\-class\-object (E0243)
\fIInvalid assignment to \(aq__class__\(aq. Should be a class definition but got a \(aq%s\(aq\fP
Used when an invalid object is assigned to a __class__ property. Only a class
is permitted.
.TP
.B invalid\-enum\-extension (E0244)
\fIExtending inherited Enum class \(dq%s\(dq\fP
Used when a class tries to extend an inherited Enum class. Doing so will
raise a TypeError at runtime.
.TP
.B declare\-non\-slot (E0245)
\fINo such name %r in __slots__\fP
Raised when a type annotation on a class is absent from the list of names in
__slots__, and __slots__ does not contain a __dict__ entry.
.TP
.B non\-iterator\-returned (E0301)
\fI__iter__ returns non\-iterator\fP
Used when an __iter__ method returns something which is not an iterable (i.e.
has no \fI__next__\fP method)
.TP
.B unexpected\-special\-method\-signature (E0302)
\fIThe special method %r expects %s param(s), %d %s given\fP
Emitted when a special method was defined with an invalid number of
parameters. If it has too few or too many, it might not work at all.
.TP
.B invalid\-length\-returned (E0303)
\fI__len__ does not return non\-negative integer\fP
Used when a __len__ method returns something which is not a non\-negative
integer
.TP
.B invalid\-bool\-returned (E0304)
\fI__bool__ does not return bool\fP
Used when a __bool__ method returns something which is not a bool
.TP
.B invalid\-index\-returned (E0305)
\fI__index__ does not return int\fP
Used when an __index__ method returns something which is not an integer
.TP
.B invalid\-repr\-returned (E0306)
\fI__repr__ does not return str\fP
Used when a __repr__ method returns something which is not a string
.TP
.B invalid\-str\-returned (E0307)
\fI__str__ does not return str\fP
Used when a __str__ method returns something which is not a string
.TP
.B invalid\-bytes\-returned (E0308)
\fI__bytes__ does not return bytes\fP
Used when a __bytes__ method returns something which is not bytes
.TP
.B invalid\-hash\-returned (E0309)
\fI__hash__ does not return int\fP
Used when a __hash__ method returns something which is not an integer
.TP
.B invalid\-length\-hint\-returned (E0310)
\fI__length_hint__ does not return non\-negative integer\fP
Used when a __length_hint__ method returns something which is not a non\-
negative integer
.TP
.B invalid\-format\-returned (E0311)
\fI__format__ does not return str\fP
Used when a __format__ method returns something which is not a string
.TP
.B invalid\-getnewargs\-returned (E0312)
\fI__getnewargs__ does not return a tuple\fP
Used when a __getnewargs__ method returns something which is not a tuple
.TP
.B invalid\-getnewargs\-ex\-returned (E0313)
\fI__getnewargs_ex__ does not return a tuple containing (tuple, dict)\fP
Used when a __getnewargs_ex__ method returns something which is not of the
form tuple(tuple, dict)
.TP
.B attribute\-defined\-outside\-init (W0201)
\fIAttribute %r defined outside __init__\fP
Used when an instance attribute is defined outside the __init__ method.
.TP
.B bad\-staticmethod\-argument (W0211)
\fIStatic method with %r as first argument\fP
Used when a static method has \(dqself\(dq or a value specified in valid\-
classmethod\-first\-arg option or valid\-metaclass\-classmethod\-first\-arg option
as first argument.
.TP
.B protected\-access (W0212)
\fIAccess to a protected member %s of a client class\fP
Used when a protected member (i.e. class member with a name beginning with an
underscore) is accessed outside the class or a descendant of the class where
it\(aqs defined.
.TP
.B implicit\-flag\-alias (W0213)
\fIFlag member %(overlap)s shares bit positions with %(sources)s\fP
Used when multiple integer values declared within an enum.IntFlag class share
a common bit position.
.TP
.B arguments\-differ (W0221)
\fI%s %s %r method\fP
Used when a method has a different number of arguments than in the
implemented interface or in an overridden method. Extra arguments with
default values are ignored.
.TP
.B signature\-differs (W0222)
\fISignature differs from %s %r method\fP
Used when a method signature is different than in the implemented interface
or in an overridden method.
.TP
.B abstract\-method (W0223)
\fIMethod %r is abstract in class %r but is not overridden in child class %r\fP
Used when an abstract method (i.e. raise NotImplementedError) is not
overridden in concrete class.
.TP
.B super\-init\-not\-called (W0231)
\fI__init__ method from base class %r is not called\fP
Used when an ancestor class method has an __init__ method which is not called
by a derived class.
.TP
.B non\-parent\-init\-called (W0233)
\fI__init__ method from a non direct base class %r is called\fP
Used when an __init__ method is called on a class which is not in the direct
ancestors for the analysed class.
.TP
.B invalid\-overridden\-method (W0236)
\fIMethod %r was expected to be %r, found it instead as %r\fP
Used when we detect that a method was overridden in a way that does not match
its base class which could result in potential bugs at runtime.
.TP
.B arguments\-renamed (W0237)
\fI%s %s %r method\fP
Used when a method parameter has a different name than in the implemented
interface or in an overridden method.
.TP
.B unused\-private\-member (W0238)
\fIUnused private member \(ga%s.%s\(ga\fP
Emitted when a private member of a class is defined but not used.
.TP
.B overridden\-final\-method (W0239)
\fIMethod %r overrides a method decorated with typing.final which is defined in class %r\fP
Used when a method decorated with typing.final has been overridden.
.TP
.B subclassed\-final\-class (W0240)
\fIClass %r is a subclass of a class decorated with typing.final: %r\fP
Used when a class decorated with typing.final has been subclassed.
.TP
.B redefined\-slots\-in\-subclass (W0244)
\fIRedefined slots %r in subclass\fP
Used when a slot is re\-defined in a subclass.
.TP
.B super\-without\-brackets (W0245)
\fISuper call without brackets\fP
Used when a call to super does not have brackets and thus is not an actual
call and does not work as expected.
.TP
.B useless\-parent\-delegation (W0246)
\fIUseless parent or super() delegation in method %r\fP
Used whenever we can detect that an overridden method is useless, relying on
parent or super() delegation to do the same thing as another method from the
MRO.
.TP
.B no\-classmethod\-decorator (R0202)
\fIConsider using a decorator instead of calling classmethod\fP
Used when a class method is defined without using the decorator syntax.
.TP
.B no\-staticmethod\-decorator (R0203)
\fIConsider using a decorator instead of calling staticmethod\fP
Used when a static method is defined without using the decorator syntax.
.TP
.B useless\-object\-inheritance (R0205)
\fIClass %r inherits from object, can be safely removed from bases in python3\fP
Used when a class inherit from object, which under python3 is implicit, hence
can be safely removed from bases.
.TP
.B property\-with\-parameters (R0206)
\fICannot have defined parameters for properties\fP
Used when we detect that a property also has parameters, which are useless,
given that properties cannot be called with additional arguments.
.TP
.B bad\-classmethod\-argument (C0202)
\fIClass method %s should have %s as first argument\fP
Used when a class method has a first argument named differently than the
value specified in valid\-classmethod\-first\-arg option (default to \(dqcls\(dq),
recommended to easily differentiate them from regular instance methods.
.TP
.B bad\-mcs\-method\-argument (C0203)
\fIMetaclass method %s should have %s as first argument\fP
Used when a metaclass method has a first argument named differently than the
value specified in valid\-classmethod\-first\-arg option (default to \(dqcls\(dq),
recommended to easily differentiate them from regular instance methods.
.TP
.B bad\-mcs\-classmethod\-argument (C0204)
\fIMetaclass class method %s should have %s as first argument\fP
Used when a metaclass class method has a first argument named differently
than the value specified in valid\-metaclass\-classmethod\-first\-arg option
(default to \(dqmcs\(dq), recommended to easily differentiate them from regular
instance methods.
.TP
.B single\-string\-used\-for\-slots (C0205)
\fIClass __slots__ should be a non\-string iterable\fP
Used when a class __slots__ is a simple string, rather than an iterable.
.TP
.B method\-check\-failed (F0202)
\fIUnable to check methods signature (%s / %s)\fP
Used when Pylint has been unable to check methods signature compatibility for
an unexpected reason. Please report this kind if you don\(aqt make sense of it.
.UNINDENT
.SS Dataclass checker
.sp
Verbatim name of the checker is \fBdataclass\fP\&.
.SS Dataclass checker Messages
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B invalid\-field\-call (E3701)
\fIInvalid usage of field(), %s\fP
The dataclasses.field() specifier should only be used as the value of an
assignment within a dataclass, or within the make_dataclass() function.
.UNINDENT
.SS Design checker
.sp
Verbatim name of the checker is \fBdesign\fP\&.
.sp
See also design checker\(aqs options\(aq documentation \%<#\:design-options>
.SS Design checker Messages
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B too\-many\-ancestors (R0901)
\fIToo many ancestors (%s/%s)\fP
Used when class has too many parent classes, try to reduce this to get a
simpler (and so easier to use) class.
.TP
.B too\-many\-instance\-attributes (R0902)
\fIToo many instance attributes (%s/%s)\fP
Used when class has too many instance attributes, try to reduce this to get a
simpler (and so easier to use) class.
.TP
.B too\-few\-public\-methods (R0903)
\fIToo few public methods (%s/%s)\fP
Used when class has too few public methods, so be sure it\(aqs really worth it.
.TP
.B too\-many\-public\-methods (R0904)
\fIToo many public methods (%s/%s)\fP
Used when class has too many public methods, try to reduce this to get a
simpler (and so easier to use) class.
.TP
.B too\-many\-return\-statements (R0911)
\fIToo many return statements (%s/%s)\fP
Used when a function or method has too many return statement, making it hard
to follow.
.TP
.B too\-many\-branches (R0912)
\fIToo many branches (%s/%s)\fP
Used when a function or method has too many branches, making it hard to
follow.
.TP
.B too\-many\-arguments (R0913)
\fIToo many arguments (%s/%s)\fP
Used when a function or method takes too many arguments.
.TP
.B too\-many\-locals (R0914)
\fIToo many local variables (%s/%s)\fP
Used when a function or method has too many local variables.
.TP
.B too\-many\-statements (R0915)
\fIToo many statements (%s/%s)\fP
Used when a function or method has too many statements. You should then split
it in smaller functions / methods.
.TP
.B too\-many\-boolean\-expressions (R0916)
\fIToo many boolean expressions in if statement (%s/%s)\fP
Used when an if statement contains too many boolean expressions.
.TP
.B too\-many\-positional\-arguments (R0917)
\fIToo many positional arguments (%s/%s)\fP
Used when a function has too many positional arguments.
.UNINDENT
.SS Exceptions checker
.sp
Verbatim name of the checker is \fBexceptions\fP\&.
.sp
See also exceptions checker\(aqs options\(aq documentation \%<#\:exceptions-options>
.SS Exceptions checker Messages
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B bad\-except\-order (E0701)
\fIBad except clauses order (%s)\fP
Used when except clauses are not in the correct order (from the more specific
to the more generic). If you don\(aqt fix the order, some exceptions may not be
caught by the most specific handler.
.TP
.B raising\-bad\-type (E0702)
\fIRaising %s while only classes or instances are allowed\fP
Used when something which is neither a class nor an instance is raised (i.e.
a \fITypeError\fP will be raised).
.TP
.B misplaced\-bare\-raise (E0704)
\fIThe raise statement is not inside an except clause\fP
Used when a bare raise is not used inside an except clause. This generates an
error, since there are no active exceptions to be reraised. An exception to
this rule is represented by a bare raise inside a finally clause, which might
work, as long as an exception is raised inside the try block, but it is
nevertheless a code smell that must not be relied upon.
.TP
.B bad\-exception\-cause (E0705)
\fIException cause set to something which is not an exception, nor None\fP
Used when using the syntax \(dqraise ... from ...\(dq, where the exception cause is
not an exception, nor None.
.TP
.B raising\-non\-exception (E0710)
\fIRaising a class which doesn\(aqt inherit from BaseException\fP
Used when a class which doesn\(aqt inherit from BaseException is raised.
.TP
.B notimplemented\-raised (E0711)
\fINotImplemented raised \- should raise NotImplementedError\fP
Used when NotImplemented is raised instead of NotImplementedError
.TP
.B catching\-non\-exception (E0712)
\fICatching an exception which doesn\(aqt inherit from Exception: %s\fP
Used when a class which doesn\(aqt inherit from Exception is used as an
exception in an except clause.
.TP
.B bare\-except (W0702)
\fINo exception type(s) specified\fP
A bare \fBexcept:\fP clause will catch \fBSystemExit\fP and \fBKeyboardInterrupt\fP
exceptions, making it harder to interrupt a program with \fBControl\-C\fP, and
can disguise other problems. If you want to catch all exceptions that signal
program errors, use \fBexcept Exception:\fP (bare except is equivalent to
\fBexcept BaseException:\fP).
.TP
.B duplicate\-except (W0705)
\fICatching previously caught exception type %s\fP
Used when an except catches a type that was already caught by a previous
handler.
.TP
.B try\-except\-raise (W0706)
\fIThe except handler raises immediately\fP
Used when an except handler uses raise as its first or only operator. This is
useless because it raises back the exception immediately. Remove the raise
operator or the entire try\-except\-raise block!
.TP
.B raise\-missing\-from (W0707)
\fIConsider explicitly re\-raising using %s\(aq%s from %s\(aq\fP
Python\(aqs exception chaining shows the traceback of the current exception, but
also of the original exception. When you raise a new exception after another
exception was caught it\(aqs likely that the second exception is a friendly re\-
wrapping of the first exception. In such cases \fIraise from\fP provides a better
link between the two tracebacks in the final error.
.TP
.B binary\-op\-exception (W0711)
\fIException to catch is the result of a binary \(dq%s\(dq operation\fP
Used when the exception to catch is of the form \(dqexcept A or B:\(dq. If
intending to catch multiple, rewrite as \(dqexcept (A, B):\(dq
.TP
.B raising\-format\-tuple (W0715)
\fIException arguments suggest string formatting might be intended\fP
Used when passing multiple arguments to an exception constructor, the first
of them a string literal containing what appears to be placeholders intended
for formatting
.TP
.B wrong\-exception\-operation (W0716)
\fIInvalid exception operation. %s\fP
Used when an operation is done against an exception, but the operation is not
valid for the exception in question. Usually emitted when having binary
operations between exceptions in except handlers.
.TP
.B broad\-exception\-caught (W0718)
\fICatching too general exception %s\fP
If you use a naked \fBexcept Exception:\fP clause, you might end up catching
exceptions other than the ones you expect to catch. This can hide bugs or
make it harder to debug programs when unrelated errors are hidden.
.TP
.B broad\-exception\-raised (W0719)
\fIRaising too general exception: %s\fP
Raising exceptions that are too generic force you to catch exceptions
generically too. It will force you to use a naked \fBexcept Exception:\fP
clause. You might then end up catching exceptions other than the ones you
expect to catch. This can hide bugs or make it harder to debug programs when
unrelated errors are hidden.
.UNINDENT
.SS Format checker
.sp
Verbatim name of the checker is \fBformat\fP\&.
.sp
See also format checker\(aqs options\(aq documentation \%<#\:format-options>
.SS Format checker Messages
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B unnecessary\-semicolon (W0301)
\fIUnnecessary semicolon\fP
Used when a statement is ended by a semi\-colon (\(dq;\(dq), which isn\(aqt necessary
(that\(aqs python, not C ;).
.TP
.B bad\-indentation (W0311)
\fIBad indentation. Found %s %s, expected %s\fP
Used when an unexpected number of indentation\(aqs tabulations or spaces has
been found.
.TP
.B line\-too\-long (C0301)
\fILine too long (%s/%s)\fP
Used when a line is longer than a given number of characters.
.TP
.B too\-many\-lines (C0302)
\fIToo many lines in module (%s/%s)\fP
Used when a module has too many lines, reducing its readability.
.TP
.B trailing\-whitespace (C0303)
\fITrailing whitespace\fP
Used when there is whitespace between the end of a line and the newline.
.TP
.B missing\-final\-newline (C0304)
\fIFinal newline missing\fP
Used when the last line in a file is missing a newline.
.TP
.B trailing\-newlines (C0305)
\fITrailing newlines\fP
Used when there are trailing blank lines in a file.
.TP
.B multiple\-statements (C0321)
\fIMore than one statement on a single line\fP
Used when more than on statement are found on the same line.
.TP
.B superfluous\-parens (C0325)
\fIUnnecessary parens after %r keyword\fP
Used when a single item in parentheses follows an if, for, or other keyword.
.TP
.B mixed\-line\-endings (C0327)
\fIMixed line endings LF and CRLF\fP
Used when there are mixed (LF and CRLF) newline signs in a file.
.TP
.B unexpected\-line\-ending\-format (C0328)
\fIUnexpected line ending format. There is \(aq%s\(aq while it should be \(aq%s\(aq.\fP
Used when there is different newline than expected.
.UNINDENT
.SS Imports checker
.sp
Verbatim name of the checker is \fBimports\fP\&.
.sp
See also imports checker\(aqs options\(aq documentation \%<#\:imports-options>
.SS Imports checker Messages
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B import\-error (E0401)
\fIUnable to import %s\fP
Used when pylint has been unable to import a module.
.TP
.B relative\-beyond\-top\-level (E0402)
\fIAttempted relative import beyond top\-level package\fP
Used when a relative import tries to access too many levels in the current
package.
.TP
.B wildcard\-import (W0401)
\fIWildcard import %s\fP
Used when \fIfrom module import *\fP is detected.
.TP
.B reimported (W0404)
\fIReimport %r (imported line %s)\fP
Used when a module is imported more than once.
.TP
.B import\-self (W0406)
\fIModule import itself\fP
Used when a module is importing itself.
.TP
.B preferred\-module (W0407)
\fIPrefer importing %r instead of %r\fP
Used when a module imported has a preferred replacement module.
.TP
.B misplaced\-future (W0410)
\fI__future__ import is not the first non docstring statement\fP
Python 2.5 and greater require __future__ import to be the first non
docstring statement in the module.
.TP
.B shadowed\-import (W0416)
\fIShadowed %r (imported line %s)\fP
Used when a module is aliased with a name that shadows another import.
.TP
.B deprecated\-module (W4901)
\fIDeprecated module %r\fP
A module marked as deprecated is imported.
.TP
.B cyclic\-import (R0401)
\fICyclic import (%s)\fP
Used when a cyclic import between two or more modules is detected.
.TP
.B consider\-using\-from\-import (R0402)
\fIUse \(aqfrom %s import %s\(aq instead\fP
Emitted when a submodule of a package is imported and aliased with the same
name, e.g., instead of \fBimport concurrent.futures as futures\fP use \fBfrom
concurrent import futures\fP\&.
.TP
.B multiple\-imports (C0410)
\fIMultiple imports on one line (%s)\fP
Used when import statement importing multiple modules is detected.
.TP
.B wrong\-import\-order (C0411)
\fI%s should be placed before %s\fP
Used when PEP8 import order is not respected (standard imports first, then
third\-party libraries, then local imports).
.TP
.B ungrouped\-imports (C0412)
\fIImports from package %s are not grouped\fP
Used when imports are not grouped by packages.
.TP
.B wrong\-import\-position (C0413)
\fIImport \(dq%s\(dq should be placed at the top of the module\fP
Used when code and imports are mixed.
.TP
.B useless\-import\-alias (C0414)
\fIImport alias does not rename original package\fP
Used when an import alias is same as original package, e.g., using import
numpy as numpy instead of import numpy as np.
.TP
.B import\-outside\-toplevel (C0415)
\fIImport outside toplevel (%s)\fP
Used when an import statement is used anywhere other than the module
toplevel. Move this import to the top of the file.
.UNINDENT
